var e=`Microsoft labels the “varies by service type” rows differently on different pages. This question does not depend on those labels.`,t=`The AZ-900 Learn path covers only pay-as-you-go; reservations, savings plans, and Spot VMs come from Azure docs.`,n=`Billing depends on the Azure Functions hosting plan, so this question does not test billing.`,r=[{id:`q1.1-01`,objectiveId:`1.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.1|On-premises datacenter`],stem:`Which statement best describes cloud computing?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Delivery of computing services, such as storage and databases, over the internet`,why:`Correct: Microsoft Learn defines cloud computing as the delivery of computing services over the internet.`},{id:`b`,text:`Running every server in a datacenter the company builds, owns, and maintains itself`,why:`That describes an on-premises datacenter, where you buy and run all the infrastructure yourself.`},{id:`c`,text:`Buying hardware up front and depreciating it over several years`,why:`That is capital expenditure on physical infrastructure, the model the cloud replaces.`},{id:`d`,text:`Running a virtual machine on an employee’s own laptop`,why:`A local VM is virtualization on your own hardware, not a service delivered over the internet.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services (compute, storage, databases, networking, and more) over the internet.`},{id:`q1.1-02`,objectiveId:`1.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A retail company expects a large traffic spike during a two-week seasonal launch. It doesn’t want to buy servers months in advance. Which characteristic of cloud computing addresses this?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Dedicated hardware in a private cloud`,why:`A private cloud still means buying and maintaining hardware, which is what the company wants to avoid.`},{id:`b`,text:`Capital expenditure planning`,why:`CapEx planning means buying capacity in advance, the opposite of what the company wants.`},{id:`c`,text:`On-demand capacity that can be released afterward`,why:`Correct: the cloud lets you provision capacity when you need it and release it when the launch ends.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure sovereign regions`,why:`Sovereign regions are isolated instances of Azure for compliance needs; they don’t address temporary demand.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Cloud computing replaces long procurement cycles with on-demand provisioning: add capacity for the launch and scale it back afterward.`},{id:`q1.1-03`,objectiveId:`1.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about cloud computing, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Cloud computing includes services such as machine learning and IoT, not only virtual machines and storage.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says cloud services expand traditional IT offerings to include IoT, machine learning, and AI.`},{id:`s2`,text:`With cloud computing, adding capacity requires waiting to buy and install new servers.`,answer:!1,why:`In the cloud you provision capacity on demand instead of waiting for new hardware.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Cloud computing is limited by the physical infrastructure of your own datacenter.`,answer:!1,why:`Because services are delivered over the internet, you aren’t constrained by your own datacenter.`}],explanation:`Cloud computing delivers a broad range of services over the internet and lets you add capacity on demand, without being limited by your own hardware.`},{id:`q1.2-01`,objectiveId:`1.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:e,covers:[`1.2|Accounts and identities vs identity and directory infrastructure`],stem:`Under the shared responsibility model, which responsibility always stays with the customer, whatever the service type?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The physical hosts in the datacenter`,why:`The physical hosts are always the cloud provider’s responsibility.`},{id:`b`,text:`The operating system`,why:`Responsibility for the operating system depends on the service type; in PaaS and SaaS the provider manages it.`},{id:`c`,text:`Network controls`,why:`Network controls depend on the service type.`},{id:`d`,text:`Accounts and identities`,why:`Correct: accounts and identities, like data and devices, always stay with the customer.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`The customer always keeps three things: the information and data, the devices that connect, and the accounts and identities in the environment.`},{id:`q1.2-02`,objectiveId:`1.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:e,stem:`Under the shared responsibility model, which two responsibilities always belong to the cloud provider? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The physical datacenter`,why:`Correct: the physical datacenter is always the provider’s responsibility.`},{id:`b`,text:`Devices that are allowed to connect to your cloud`,why:`Connected devices always stay with the customer.`},{id:`c`,text:`Information and data stored in the cloud`,why:`Data always stays with the customer.`},{id:`d`,text:`The physical network`,why:`Correct: the physical network is always the provider’s responsibility.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`The provider always owns the physical layer: the physical datacenter, the physical network, and the physical hosts.`},{id:`q1.2-03`,objectiveId:`1.2`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:e,covers:[`1.2|Accounts and identities vs identity and directory infrastructure`],stem:`Match each item to who is responsible for it under the shared responsibility model. Each answer may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Physical hosts`,why:`The physical hosts are always the provider’s.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Accounts and identities`,why:`Accounts and identities always stay with the customer.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Operating systems`,why:`OS responsibility shifts from the customer (IaaS) to the provider (PaaS, SaaS).`},{id:`i4`,text:`Devices that connect to your cloud`,why:`Connected devices always stay with the customer.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Always the cloud provider`},{id:`t2`,text:`Always the customer`},{id:`t3`,text:`Depends on the service type`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t2`},explanation:`The provider always owns the physical layer, the customer always owns data, devices, and accounts, and items like the OS depend on IaaS, PaaS, or SaaS.`},{id:`q1.2-04`,objectiveId:`1.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,verify:e,covers:[`1.2|SaaS means the provider handles everything`],stem:`A company moves its email to a SaaS product. For each statement, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`The company is still responsible for the data stored in its mailboxes.`,answer:!0,why:`Data always stays with the customer, even in SaaS.`},{id:`s2`,text:`The provider is responsible for the physical security of the datacenter.`,answer:!0,why:`The physical datacenter is always the provider’s responsibility.`},{id:`s3`,text:`The company no longer has any responsibility for its user accounts.`,answer:!1,why:`Accounts and identities always stay with the customer.`}],explanation:`SaaS moves the most responsibility to the provider, but the customer never hands off its data, devices, or accounts and identities.`},{id:`q1.3-01`,objectiveId:`1.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which cloud model is built, controlled, and maintained by a third-party provider and available to anyone who wants to buy services?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Public cloud`,why:`Correct: general public availability is the key difference between public and private clouds.`},{id:`b`,text:`Private cloud`,why:`A private cloud is used by a single organization.`},{id:`c`,text:`Hybrid cloud`,why:`Hybrid cloud combines public and private clouds in one environment.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure sovereign region`,why:`Sovereign regions are isolated instances of Azure for compliance or legal needs, not a general cloud model.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`A public cloud is built, controlled, and maintained by a third-party provider, and anyone can buy its services.`},{id:`q1.3-02`,objectiveId:`1.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.3|Multicloud`,`1.4|Hybrid for any mix of clouds`],stem:`A company runs some workloads in Azure and others with a second public cloud provider. It has no private cloud. Which term describes this setup?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Hybrid cloud`,why:`Hybrid means public and private clouds together; this company has no private cloud.`},{id:`b`,text:`Private cloud`,why:`Both environments are public clouds, not single-organization private clouds.`},{id:`c`,text:`Multicloud`,why:`Correct: multicloud means using two or more public cloud providers.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure VMware Solution`,why:`Azure VMware Solution runs VMware workloads in Azure; it isn’t a name for a multi-provider setup.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Using two or more public cloud providers is multicloud. Hybrid always involves a private cloud.`},{id:`q1.3-03`,objectiveId:`1.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.3|Private cloud = on-premises only`],stem:`For each statement about cloud models, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A private cloud can be hosted in a dedicated offsite datacenter run by a third party.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says a private cloud may be hosted on-site or in a dedicated offsite datacenter, even by a third party.`},{id:`s2`,text:`In a private cloud, your data is colocated with other tenants’ data.`,answer:!1,why:`A private cloud is used by a single entity, so data isn’t colocated with other tenants’ data.`},{id:`s3`,text:`A hybrid cloud always uses two or more public cloud providers.`,answer:!1,why:`Two or more public providers is multicloud; hybrid combines public and private clouds.`}],explanation:`Single-organization use makes a cloud private, wherever it’s hosted. Hybrid connects public and private clouds; multiple public providers is multicloud.`},{id:`q1.3-04`,objectiveId:`1.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Azure ___ helps you manage public, private, hybrid, and multicloud environments.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Migrate`,why:`Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure; it doesn’t manage environments across clouds.`},{id:`b`,text:`Advisor`,why:`Azure Advisor gives recommendations for Azure resources, not cross-cloud management.`},{id:`c`,text:`Data Box`,why:`Azure Data Box is a physical device for transferring large amounts of data.`},{id:`d`,text:`Arc`,why:`Correct: Azure Arc manages cloud environments whether they are public, private, hybrid, or multicloud.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Microsoft Learn describes Azure Arc as a set of technologies that manages public, private, hybrid, and multicloud environments.`},{id:`q1.4-01`,objectiveId:`1.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`An organization runs a private cloud but needs extra capacity for a few weeks each year. It wants to keep its private cloud and add capacity temporarily without buying hardware. Which cloud model fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Hybrid cloud`,why:`Correct: hybrid cloud lets a private cloud surge into the public cloud for temporary demand.`},{id:`b`,text:`Private cloud, adding more on-premises servers`,why:`Adding servers means buying hardware, which the organization wants to avoid.`},{id:`c`,text:`Multicloud`,why:`Multicloud means two or more public providers; it doesn’t describe extending a private cloud.`},{id:`d`,text:`Public cloud, moving every workload off the private cloud`,why:`The organization wants to keep its private cloud, not abandon it.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Learn gives this exact hybrid use case: let a private cloud surge into public cloud resources for increased, temporary demand.`},{id:`q1.4-02`,objectiveId:`1.4`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.4|Hybrid for any mix of clouds`],stem:`Match each requirement to the cloud model that fits it best. Each model may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`No up-front hardware costs; pay only for what you use`,why:`Public cloud has no capital expenditure to scale and charges only for what you use.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Complete control over resources and security, with data not colocated with other tenants`,why:`Private cloud gives complete control and single-tenant data.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Keep some services private for compliance while running others in a public cloud`,why:`Hybrid cloud lets you choose which services stay private and which run in public cloud.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Use features from two different public cloud providers`,why:`Using two or more public providers is multicloud.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Public cloud`},{id:`t2`,text:`Private cloud`},{id:`t3`,text:`Hybrid cloud`},{id:`t4`,text:`Multicloud`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Match the model to the requirement: public for pay-per-use with no hardware, private for full control, hybrid to mix private and public, multicloud for several public providers.`},{id:`q1.4-03`,objectiveId:`1.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company already runs its workloads on VMware in its own datacenter. It wants to run those VMware workloads in Azure with seamless integration. Which should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Azure Arc manages resources across environments; it doesn’t run VMware workloads in Azure.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets`,why:`Scale sets run groups of identical Azure VMs; they don’t run your existing VMware environment in Azure.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Azure Data Box ships large amounts of data physically; it doesn’t run workloads.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure VMware Solution`,why:`Correct: Azure VMware Solution lets you run VMware workloads in Azure.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`For an organization already established with VMware, Learn names Azure VMware Solution as the way to run those workloads in Azure.`},{id:`q1.5-01`,objectiveId:`1.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.5|CapEx`],stem:`Which statement correctly describes capital expenditure (CapEx) and operational expenditure (OpEx)?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`CapEx is ongoing spending on services; OpEx is up-front spending on hardware`,why:`This reverses the two definitions.`},{id:`b`,text:`Both terms describe paying only for the resources you use`,why:`Only OpEx matches paying as you consume; CapEx is an up-front purchase.`},{id:`c`,text:`CapEx is up-front spending on physical infrastructure; OpEx is ongoing spending on services`,why:`Correct: this matches Learn’s definitions.`},{id:`d`,text:`OpEx applies only to on-premises datacenters`,why:`Cloud computing is classified as OpEx, so OpEx is not limited to on-premises.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`CapEx is up-front spending on physical infrastructure; OpEx is ongoing spending on services over time. Cloud computing is OpEx.`},{id:`q1.5-02`,objectiveId:`1.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A startup doesn’t want to spend money on servers before it knows how much demand it will have. Which benefit of the consumption-based model helps most?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`No up-front hardware costs`,why:`Correct: with the consumption-based model you rent what you need instead of buying servers first.`},{id:`b`,text:`The same fixed monthly bill regardless of usage`,why:`Consumption-based pricing varies with usage; it isn’t a fixed bill.`},{id:`c`,text:`Dedicated hardware`,why:`That describes a private cloud, which requires buying hardware.`},{id:`d`,text:`Automatic migration of on-premises servers`,why:`Migration is not a benefit of the consumption-based model.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`The consumption-based model has no up-front hardware costs and no need to buy capacity that may go unused.`},{id:`q1.5-03`,objectiveId:`1.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`1.5|CapEx`],stem:`For each statement about the consumption-based model, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`You can release resources when demand decreases and stop paying for them.`,answer:!0,why:`You pay only for what you use and can release resources when you’re done.`},{id:`s2`,text:`In a traditional datacenter, underestimating demand has no effect on application performance.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says underestimating demand means your applications suffer degraded performance.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Cloud computing is classified as a capital expenditure (CapEx).`,answer:!1,why:`Because you pay as you consume, cloud computing is an operational expense (OpEx).`}],explanation:`In the cloud you pay only for what you use and avoid the over- and under-estimating problems of a traditional datacenter; spending is OpEx.`},{id:`q1.6-01`,objectiveId:`1.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,verify:t,covers:[`1.6|Reservations vs savings plan`],stem:`A company will run the same VM size in the same region around the clock for the next three years. It wants a discount in exchange for committing to that specific resource. Which pricing option fits?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Spot Virtual Machines`,why:`Spot VMs can be evicted at any time, so they don’t suit an always-on workload.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Reservations`,why:`Correct: a reservation commits to a specific resource, size, and region for one or three years, and suits stable usage.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure savings plan for compute`,why:`A savings plan commits to an hourly spend that applies flexibly across services and regions, not a specific resource.`},{id:`d`,text:`Pay-as-you-go`,why:`Pay-as-you-go has no commitment, so there is no commitment-based discount.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Reservations lock in a discount for a specific resource, size, and region, which suits stable, predictable usage.`},{id:`q1.6-02`,objectiveId:`1.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,verify:t,stem:`A team runs batch processing jobs that can be interrupted and restarted. It wants low-cost compute that uses Azure’s unused capacity. Which should it choose?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Reservations`,why:`Reservations suit stable, always-on usage and require a one- or three-year commitment.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Dedicated Host`,why:`A dedicated host isn’t described as using unused capacity at a discount.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Spot Virtual Machines`,why:`Correct: Spot VMs use unused capacity at a significant discount and suit interruptible work like batch jobs.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure savings plan for compute`,why:`A savings plan is a spend commitment, not a way to use spare capacity.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Spot VMs run on Azure’s unused capacity at a discount but can be evicted, so they fit workloads that handle interruptions.`},{id:`q1.6-03`,objectiveId:`1.6`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:t,covers:[`1.6|Reservations vs savings plan`],stem:`Match each description to the pricing option it describes. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`No commitment; pay only for what you consume`,why:`That is pay-as-you-go.`},{id:`i2`,text:`One- or three-year commitment to a specific resource, size, and region`,why:`That is a reservation.`},{id:`i3`,text:`One- or three-year hourly spend commitment across eligible compute services`,why:`That is a savings plan for compute.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Unused capacity at a discount that can be evicted`,why:`That is Spot Virtual Machines.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Pay-as-you-go`},{id:`t2`,text:`Azure Reservations`},{id:`t3`,text:`Azure savings plan for compute`},{id:`t4`,text:`Azure Spot Virtual Machines`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Pay-as-you-go has no commitment; reservations lock a specific resource; savings plans commit an hourly spend; Spot uses evictable spare capacity.`},{id:`q1.7-01`,objectiveId:`1.7`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:n,stem:`Azure ___ is an event-driven, serverless compute option that runs code when an event triggers it.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Virtual Machines`,why:`VMs must stay running for your app to work; they aren’t serverless.`},{id:`b`,text:`Functions`,why:`Correct: Azure Functions is Azure’s event-driven, serverless compute option.`},{id:`c`,text:`Virtual Desktop`,why:`Azure Virtual Desktop delivers Windows desktops and apps, not event-driven code.`},{id:`d`,text:`Kubernetes Service`,why:`AKS orchestrates containers, which you keep running; it isn’t Learn’s serverless example.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure Functions runs code when an event wakes it, with no VMs or containers for you to maintain.`},{id:`q1.7-02`,objectiveId:`1.7`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:n,stem:`For each statement about serverless computing, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`With serverless, you don’t provision or maintain the VMs or containers that run your code.`,answer:!0,why:`Serverless means the provider manages the infrastructure for you.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Serverless means the code runs without any servers at all.`,answer:!1,why:`Serverless code still runs on servers; you just don’t manage them.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Azure Functions are stateless by default, and Durable Functions add state.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says functions are stateless by default; Durable Functions are the stateful option.`}],explanation:`Serverless still uses servers, but the provider manages them. Functions are stateless unless you use Durable Functions.`},{id:`q1.7-03`,objectiveId:`1.7`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,verify:n,covers:[`1.7|Virtual machines and containers`],stem:`A developer needs code to run each time a message arrives from another Azure service, and demand is unpredictable. Which two characteristics of Azure Functions make it a good fit? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`It must stay running continuously to respond to messages`,why:`That describes VMs and containers; a function runs only when an event wakes it.`},{id:`b`,text:`It runs when triggered by an event, such as a message`,why:`Correct: an event such as a message wakes the function.`},{id:`c`,text:`You control the operating system and installed software`,why:`That describes a VM (IaaS), not serverless.`},{id:`d`,text:`It scales automatically based on demand`,why:`Correct: Functions scale automatically, which suits variable demand.`}],answer:[`b`,`d`],explanation:`Azure Functions is event-driven and scales automatically, so it fits unpredictable, message-triggered work.`}],i=[{id:`q2.1-01`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A developer’s virtual machine keeps running out of memory, so they increase its RAM and add more CPUs. What is this an example of?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Horizontal scaling`,why:`Horizontal scaling changes the number of resources, not the size of one.`},{id:`b`,text:`High availability`,why:`High availability is about staying up despite disruptions, not about resizing a VM.`},{id:`c`,text:`Elasticity`,why:`Elasticity is automatic, real-time adjustment; this was a deliberate resize.`},{id:`d`,text:`Vertical scaling`,why:`Correct: vertical scaling (scaling up) increases the capabilities of a resource, such as CPUs or RAM.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Vertical scaling changes a resource’s capabilities. Adding CPUs or RAM to a VM is scaling up.`},{id:`q2.1-02`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A web app suddenly gets heavy traffic, so the team adds three more virtual machines to share the load. What type of scaling is this?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Scaling up`,why:`Scaling up is vertical: making one resource bigger.`},{id:`b`,text:`Scaling out`,why:`Correct: adding more VMs is horizontal scaling, also called scaling out.`},{id:`c`,text:`Scaling down`,why:`Scaling down reduces a resource’s CPUs or RAM.`},{id:`d`,text:`Scaling in`,why:`Scaling in removes resources when demand drops.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Horizontal scaling adds or removes resources. Adding VMs is scaling out; removing them is scaling in.`},{id:`q2.1-03`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`microsoft`,covers:[`2.1|High availability vs scalability`,`2.1|Scalability vs elasticity`],stem:`Match each description to the cloud concept it describes. Each concept may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Increase the CPU and RAM of a virtual machine`,why:`Changing a resource’s capabilities is vertical scaling.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Add more VM instances as demand grows`,why:`Changing the number of resources is horizontal scaling.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Resources adjust automatically, in real time, to actual demand`,why:`Automatic, real-time adjustment is elasticity (Microsoft’s cloud computing dictionary).`},{id:`i4`,text:`Keep a service available regardless of disruptions`,why:`Maximum availability despite disruptions is high availability.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Vertical scaling`},{id:`t2`,text:`Horizontal scaling`},{id:`t3`,text:`Elasticity`},{id:`t4`,text:`High availability`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Vertical scaling resizes, horizontal scaling changes the count, elasticity adjusts automatically in real time, and high availability keeps services up.`},{id:`q2.1-04`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Where are Azure’s uptime guarantees for a service defined?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`In service-level agreements (SLAs)`,why:`Correct: Azure’s uptime guarantees are part of its service-level agreements.`},{id:`b`,text:`In Azure Policy definitions`,why:`Azure Policy enforces rules on your resources; it doesn’t define Microsoft’s uptime guarantees.`},{id:`c`,text:`In Azure Advisor reliability recommendations`,why:`Advisor suggests improvements; it isn’t where guarantees are defined.`},{id:`d`,text:`In the pricing calculator`,why:`The pricing calculator estimates costs, not availability.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Azure is highly available, with uptime guarantees that depend on the service and are defined in SLAs.`},{id:`q2.1-05`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`microsoft`,covers:[`2.1|Scalability vs elasticity`],stem:`For each statement about scaling, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Elasticity is another name for scaling up a single virtual machine.`,answer:!1,why:`Elasticity is automatic, real-time adjustment to demand; scaling up is vertical scaling.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Because the cloud is consumption-based, removing resources when demand drops reduces cost.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says reducing resources when demand drops reduces your costs.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Horizontal scaling can happen manually or automatically.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says resources can be scaled out or in either automatically or manually.`}],explanation:`Scaling adjusts resources to demand, manually or automatically; elasticity is the automatic, real-time version.`},{id:`q2.1-06`,objectiveId:`2.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Removing virtual machines when demand drops is called scaling ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`up`,why:`Scaling up adds capabilities to one resource.`},{id:`b`,text:`down`,why:`Scaling down lowers a resource’s CPU or RAM (vertical), not the number of VMs.`},{id:`c`,text:`in`,why:`Correct: removing resources is scaling in (horizontal).`},{id:`d`,text:`out`,why:`Scaling out adds resources.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Horizontal scaling uses out (add) and in (remove); vertical scaling uses up and down.`},{id:`q2.2-01`,objectiveId:`2.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which term describes the ability of a system to recover from failures and continue to function?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Predictability`,why:`Predictability is about forecasting performance and cost.`},{id:`b`,text:`Scalability`,why:`Scalability adjusts resources to meet demand.`},{id:`c`,text:`Reliability`,why:`Correct: Learn defines reliability as the ability to recover from failures and continue to function.`},{id:`d`,text:`Manageability`,why:`Manageability is about how you manage resources and which tools you use.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Reliability is the ability to recover from failures and keep working. It’s a pillar of the Azure Well-Architected Framework.`},{id:`q2.2-02`,objectiveId:`2.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A finance team wants to forecast next quarter’s cloud spend and estimate costs before deploying new resources. Which cloud benefit does this describe?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Cost predictability`,why:`Correct: cost predictability means forecasting cloud spend, for example with the Pricing Calculator.`},{id:`b`,text:`Performance predictability`,why:`Performance predictability is about the resources needed for a good customer experience.`},{id:`c`,text:`Reliability`,why:`Reliability is about recovering from failures.`},{id:`d`,text:`High availability`,why:`High availability is about uptime.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Cost predictability means predicting cloud spend: tracking usage, analyzing trends, and estimating with the pricing calculator.`},{id:`q2.2-03`,objectiveId:`2.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`According to Microsoft Learn, which two cloud concepts support performance predictability? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Resource locks`,why:`Resource locks prevent accidental changes or deletion; they don’t support performance predictability.`},{id:`b`,text:`Autoscaling`,why:`Correct: autoscaling deploys extra resources when demand rises.`},{id:`c`,text:`Load balancing`,why:`Correct: load balancing redirects overload to less stressed areas.`},{id:`d`,text:`The pricing calculator`,why:`The pricing calculator supports cost predictability, not performance.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`Learn lists autoscaling, load balancing, and high availability as concepts that support performance predictability.`},{id:`q2.2-04`,objectiveId:`2.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`2.2|Reliability vs predictability`],stem:`For each statement, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Deploying resources in multiple regions supports reliability, because other regions keep running if one fails.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says that with global scale, other regions keep running even if one has a catastrophic event.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Reliability is one of the pillars of the Azure Well-Architected Framework.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn states this directly.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Predictability is mainly about recovering from failures.`,answer:!1,why:`Recovering from failures is reliability; predictability is about knowing performance and cost in advance.`}],explanation:`Reliability means recovering from failure (helped by global regions); predictability means forecasting performance and cost.`},{id:`q2.2-05`,objectiveId:`2.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`2.2|Reliability vs high availability`],stem:`Which statement correctly contrasts high availability and reliability?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`They are the same benefit with two names`,why:`Learn defines them separately: one is about uptime, the other about recovering from failure.`},{id:`b`,text:`High availability is about forecasting cost; reliability is about uptime`,why:`Forecasting cost is cost predictability, not high availability.`},{id:`c`,text:`Reliability applies only to storage, and high availability only to virtual machines`,why:`Neither benefit is limited to one type of resource.`},{id:`d`,text:`High availability focuses on uptime; reliability focuses on recovering from failures`,why:`Correct: this matches the two definitions on Microsoft Learn.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`High availability is about maximum uptime; reliability is about recovering from failures and continuing to function.`},{id:`q2.3-01`,objectiveId:`2.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants maximum control over security, including managing the operating system and installed software and applying its own patches. Which cloud service type fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`IaaS`,why:`Correct: IaaS gives maximum control, and you manage the OS, installed software, patches, and maintenance.`},{id:`b`,text:`PaaS`,why:`In PaaS the provider handles OS patches and maintenance for you.`},{id:`c`,text:`SaaS`,why:`In SaaS the provider manages almost the whole stack, including patching.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Functions`,why:`Functions is serverless: you don’t manage the operating system at all.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Learn says that if you want maximum control of security, IaaS lets you manage the OS and installed software, including patching.`},{id:`q2.3-02`,objectiveId:`2.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants software patches and maintenance to be handled for it automatically. Which two cloud service types should it consider? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)`,why:`In IaaS you handle patches and maintenance yourself.`},{id:`b`,text:`Platform as a service (PaaS)`,why:`Correct: PaaS takes care of patches and maintenance for you.`},{id:`c`,text:`An on-premises datacenter`,why:`On-premises, you are responsible for everything, including patches.`},{id:`d`,text:`Software as a service (SaaS)`,why:`Correct: SaaS also handles patches and maintenance for you.`}],answer:[`b`,`d`],explanation:`Learn says PaaS or SaaS deployments may be the best strategies if you want patches and maintenance handled automatically.`},{id:`q2.3-03`,objectiveId:`2.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about security and governance in the cloud, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Cloud-based auditing can flag resources that are out of compliance with your baseline.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says auditing flags non-compliant resources and suggests mitigation strategies.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Templates are used only for cost reporting and can’t help enforce technical standards.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says templates help ensure deployed resources meet your technical standards and regulatory requirements.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Cloud providers are poorly suited to handle distributed denial of service (DDoS) attacks.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says cloud providers are typically well suited to handle DDoS attacks.`}],explanation:`Cloud features such as templates and auditing support governance, and providers are well suited to handle DDoS attacks.`},{id:`q2.3-04`,objectiveId:`2.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`2.3|Security vs governance`],stem:`Which statement best distinguishes security from governance in the cloud?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Governance protects resources from attacks; security sets compliance baselines`,why:`This reverses the two ideas.`},{id:`b`,text:`Security protects resources from threats; governance keeps them in line with your standards`,why:`Correct: governance is about standards and compliance; security is about protecting against threats.`},{id:`c`,text:`Security applies only to IaaS, and governance only to SaaS`,why:`Learn says cloud features support governance whether you use IaaS or SaaS, and security choices exist in every model.`},{id:`d`,text:`Governance is handled entirely by the cloud provider`,why:`You set the standards and baselines; the cloud provides tools like templates and auditing.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Security protects resources from threats such as DDoS attacks; governance keeps resources compliant with your standards and regulations.`},{id:`q2.3-06`,objectiveId:`2.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company updates its technical standards for all deployed resources. In the cloud, ___ let it bring existing resources up to the new standard at scale.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`templates`,why:`Correct: Learn says templates help deployed resources meet your standards, and when standards change you can update resources at scale.`},{id:`b`,text:`availability zones`,why:`Availability zones protect against a datacenter failure; they don’t apply standards to resources.`},{id:`c`,text:`service-level agreements`,why:`SLAs describe the provider’s uptime guarantees, not your own standards.`},{id:`d`,text:`consumption-based pricing`,why:`Consumption-based pricing is how you pay; it doesn’t enforce or update standards.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Templates are a governance feature: they keep deployed resources in line with your standards, and when the standards change, resources can be updated at scale.`},{id:`q2.4-01`,objectiveId:`2.4`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`2.4|Management of vs management in the cloud`],stem:`Match each example to the type of cloud manageability it belongs to. Each type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Automatically scale resources based on need`,why:`What you can do to resources is management of the cloud.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Manage resources through a web portal`,why:`How you manage your environment is management in the cloud.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Monitor health and automatically replace failing resources`,why:`This is something you do to resources: management of the cloud.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Use PowerShell or a command-line interface`,why:`These are tools: management in the cloud.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Management of the cloud`},{id:`t2`,text:`Management in the cloud`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t1`,i4:`t2`},explanation:`Management of the cloud is what you can do to resources (scale, deploy from templates, monitor, alert); management in the cloud is how (portal, CLI, APIs, PowerShell).`},{id:`q2.4-02`,objectiveId:`2.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which is an example of management IN the cloud, meaning how you manage your environment?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Receiving automatic alerts based on configured metrics`,why:`Alerts are listed under management of the cloud.`},{id:`b`,text:`Deploying resources from a preconfigured template`,why:`Template deployment is listed under management of the cloud.`},{id:`c`,text:`Managing resources through APIs`,why:`Correct: APIs are one of the ways you manage the environment, alongside a portal, CLI, and PowerShell.`},{id:`d`,text:`Automatically replacing failing resources`,why:`Replacing failing resources is listed under management of the cloud.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Management in the cloud covers the tools: a web portal, a command-line interface, APIs, and PowerShell.`},{id:`q2.4-03`,objectiveId:`2.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about manageability in the cloud, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Deploying resources from a preconfigured template removes the need for manual configuration.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists this as a management-of-the-cloud benefit.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Management in the cloud refers to tools such as a web portal, a CLI, APIs, or PowerShell.`,answer:!0,why:`That is how Learn defines management in the cloud.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Receiving alerts based on configured metrics is an example of management in the cloud.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn lists automatic alerts under management of the cloud.`}],explanation:`Templates and alerts are management of the cloud; portals, CLIs, APIs, and PowerShell are management in the cloud.`},{id:`q2.4-04`,objectiveId:`2.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`An operations team wants resource health to be monitored and failing resources to be replaced automatically. Which cloud benefit does this describe?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Governance`,why:`Governance is about keeping resources compliant with standards.`},{id:`b`,text:`Manageability`,why:`Correct: Learn lists monitoring health and automatically replacing failing resources under manageability.`},{id:`c`,text:`Predictability`,why:`Predictability is about forecasting performance and cost.`},{id:`d`,text:`Scalability`,why:`Scalability adjusts capacity to demand; it isn’t about replacing failed resources.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Manageability (management of the cloud) includes monitoring resource health and automatically replacing failing resources.`}],a=[{id:`q3.1-01`,objectiveId:`3.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants the most control over its cloud resources, including network, database, and storage configuration. Which cloud service type fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`SaaS`,why:`SaaS is the least flexible type; the provider runs almost everything.`},{id:`b`,text:`PaaS`,why:`PaaS hands the OS and platform to the provider, so you get less control.`},{id:`c`,text:`IaaS`,why:`Correct: IaaS is the most flexible type and gives the most control.`},{id:`d`,text:`Serverless computing`,why:`Serverless removes infrastructure management entirely.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`IaaS provides the maximum control: you manage everything above the hardware, network connectivity, and physical security.`},{id:`q3.1-02`,objectiveId:`3.1`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In an IaaS model, which two are the customer’s responsibility? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Operating system installation, configuration, and maintenance`,why:`Correct: in IaaS the customer manages the OS.`},{id:`b`,text:`Physical security of the datacenter`,why:`In IaaS the provider is responsible for physical security.`},{id:`c`,text:`Network connectivity to the internet`,why:`In IaaS the provider maintains network connectivity to the internet.`},{id:`d`,text:`Database and storage configuration`,why:`Correct: Learn lists database and storage configuration as the customer’s responsibility in IaaS.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`In IaaS the provider handles hardware, internet connectivity, and physical security; you handle the OS, network configuration, and database and storage configuration.`},{id:`q3.1-06`,objectiveId:`3.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In an IaaS model, which of these does the cloud provider maintain?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Configuration of your virtual networks`,why:`In IaaS, network configuration is the customer’s job; only connectivity to the internet is the provider’s.`},{id:`b`,text:`Operating system updates on your VMs`,why:`In IaaS, installing, configuring, and maintaining the OS is the customer’s job.`},{id:`c`,text:`Network connectivity to the internet`,why:`Correct: Learn lists hardware, network connectivity to the internet, and physical security as the provider’s responsibilities in IaaS.`},{id:`d`,text:`Database and storage configuration`,why:`Learn lists database and storage configuration as the customer’s responsibility in IaaS.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`In IaaS the provider keeps the hardware, internet connectivity, and physical security running. Network configuration is different: that’s yours, along with the OS and database and storage configuration.`},{id:`q3.1-04`,objectiveId:`3.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about infrastructure as a service (IaaS), select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`In IaaS, the cloud provider maintains the hardware and physical security.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says the provider is responsible for the hardware, internet connectivity, and physical security.`},{id:`s2`,text:`IaaS gives the customer the least operational responsibility of the three service types.`,answer:!1,why:`IaaS carries the largest operational responsibility; SaaS carries the least.`},{id:`s3`,text:`With IaaS, you’re essentially renting hardware in a cloud datacenter.`,answer:!0,why:`That is how Learn describes IaaS.`}],explanation:`IaaS means renting hardware: the provider runs the physical layer, and you carry the most responsibility for everything above it.`},{id:`q3.1-05`,objectiveId:`3.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`3.1|PaaS`],stem:`Compared with PaaS, IaaS makes the customer responsible for ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`the operating system`,why:`Correct: with IaaS you manage the OS; with PaaS the provider does.`},{id:`b`,text:`the physical network`,why:`The physical network is the provider’s in both models.`},{id:`c`,text:`the physical datacenter`,why:`The physical datacenter is always the provider’s.`},{id:`d`,text:`the physical hosts`,why:`The physical hosts are always the provider’s.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`The key IaaS-versus-PaaS difference is the operating system (plus middleware and runtime), which PaaS hands to the provider.`},{id:`q3.2-01`,objectiveId:`3.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which cloud service type is the middle ground between renting infrastructure and paying for a complete, deployed solution?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)`,why:`IaaS is renting the infrastructure itself.`},{id:`b`,text:`Software as a service (SaaS)`,why:`SaaS is the complete, deployed solution.`},{id:`c`,text:`An on-premises datacenter`,why:`On-premises isn’t a cloud service type at all.`},{id:`d`,text:`Platform as a service (PaaS)`,why:`Correct: Learn describes PaaS as the middle ground between IaaS and SaaS.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`PaaS sits between IaaS (renting infrastructure) and SaaS (a finished solution).`},{id:`q3.2-02`,objectiveId:`3.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A development team wants to build and deploy its own web application without licensing or patching operating systems and databases. Which cloud service type fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)`,why:`With IaaS the team would still license and patch the OS.`},{id:`b`,text:`Platform as a service (PaaS)`,why:`Correct: PaaS provides the platform, so you don’t license or patch the OS or databases.`},{id:`c`,text:`Software as a service (SaaS)`,why:`SaaS is a finished app to use, not a platform to build your own.`},{id:`d`,text:`A private cloud`,why:`A private cloud is a deployment model, and it would still need the team to manage its infrastructure.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`PaaS gives developers a complete environment to build their own apps while the provider maintains the OS and databases.`},{id:`q3.2-03`,objectiveId:`3.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`3.2|SaaS`],stem:`For each statement about platform as a service (PaaS), select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`In PaaS, the provider maintains the operating systems, middleware, and development tools.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists these as the provider’s responsibility in PaaS.`},{id:`s2`,text:`In PaaS, the provider manages your application code and data.`,answer:!1,why:`In PaaS you still focus on your application code, data, and access controls.`},{id:`s3`,text:`PaaS gives you a finished application to use, such as email.`,answer:!1,why:`A finished application is SaaS; PaaS is a platform to build your own.`}],explanation:`PaaS is a platform you build on; SaaS is a finished application you use.`},{id:`q3.2-04`,objectiveId:`3.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`According to Microsoft Learn, which two scenarios are common uses of PaaS? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Lift-and-shift migration`,why:`Lift-and-shift is a common IaaS scenario.`},{id:`b`,text:`A development framework for building or customizing cloud apps`,why:`Correct: Learn lists this PaaS scenario.`},{id:`c`,text:`Email and messaging`,why:`Email and messaging are common SaaS scenarios.`},{id:`d`,text:`Analytics or business intelligence`,why:`Correct: Learn lists this PaaS scenario.`}],answer:[`b`,`d`],explanation:`Learn’s PaaS scenarios are development frameworks and analytics or business intelligence.`},{id:`q3.2-05`,objectiveId:`3.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In a PaaS environment, you don’t have to worry about licensing or ___ for operating systems and databases.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`data classification`,why:`Your data stays your responsibility in PaaS.`},{id:`b`,text:`access controls`,why:`Access controls stay your responsibility in PaaS.`},{id:`c`,text:`patching`,why:`Correct: Learn says you don’t have to worry about licensing or patching for OSs and databases.`},{id:`d`,text:`application code`,why:`Your application code stays your responsibility in PaaS.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`In PaaS the provider licenses and patches the OS and databases; you keep your code, data, and access controls.`},{id:`q3.3-01`,objectiveId:`3.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which is a common example of software as a service (SaaS)?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`An email and messaging application`,why:`Correct: Learn lists email and messaging as common SaaS examples.`},{id:`b`,text:`A virtual machine you configure yourself`,why:`A VM you configure is IaaS.`},{id:`c`,text:`A framework for building your own cloud apps`,why:`A development framework is PaaS.`},{id:`d`,text:`Rented hardware in a cloud datacenter`,why:`Renting hardware is IaaS.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`SaaS is a fully developed application you use, such as email, financial software, or messaging.`},{id:`q3.3-02`,objectiveId:`3.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`3.3|The provider handles everything`],stem:`A company adopts a SaaS finance application. For each statement, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`The company must patch the application’s servers.`,answer:!1,why:`In SaaS the provider handles infrastructure patching and application updates.`},{id:`s2`,text:`The company still manages its users’ access to the application.`,answer:!0,why:`In SaaS you still manage identity and access settings.`},{id:`s3`,text:`SaaS needs the least technical knowledge to get up and running.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says SaaS requires the least technical knowledge or expertise.`}],explanation:`SaaS has the lowest operational overhead, but you still manage your data, identities and access, and devices.`},{id:`q3.3-03`,objectiveId:`3.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which statement about SaaS is correct?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`It is the most flexible cloud service type`,why:`IaaS is the most flexible; SaaS is the least.`},{id:`b`,text:`The customer manages the operating system`,why:`In SaaS the provider manages almost the whole stack, including the OS.`},{id:`c`,text:`It requires the most technical expertise to use`,why:`SaaS requires the least technical knowledge.`},{id:`d`,text:`It is the least flexible type, but the easiest to get running`,why:`Correct: Learn says SaaS may be the least flexible but is the easiest to get up and running.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`SaaS trades flexibility for ease: it’s the least flexible model but the easiest to start using.`},{id:`q3.3-04`,objectiveId:`3.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`3.3|The provider handles everything`],stem:`In a SaaS model, which two remain the customer’s responsibility? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Application patching and updates`,why:`The provider handles application maintenance in SaaS.`},{id:`b`,text:`The data stored in the application`,why:`Correct: data always stays with the customer.`},{id:`c`,text:`Identity and access settings`,why:`Correct: Learn says SaaS customers primarily manage their data, identity and access settings, and device access.`},{id:`d`,text:`The physical infrastructure`,why:`The physical infrastructure is always the provider’s.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`Even in SaaS, the customer manages its data, identity and access settings, and device access.`},{id:`q3.3-05`,objectiveId:`3.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`From a product perspective, ___ is the most complete cloud service model.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`IaaS`,why:`IaaS gives you infrastructure to build on, not a complete product.`},{id:`b`,text:`SaaS`,why:`Correct: Learn calls SaaS the most complete cloud service model from a product perspective.`},{id:`c`,text:`PaaS`,why:`PaaS gives you a platform, not a finished application.`},{id:`d`,text:`a private cloud`,why:`A private cloud is a deployment model, not a service type.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`With SaaS you rent or use a fully developed application, the most complete service model.`},{id:`q3.4-01`,objectiveId:`3.4`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`3.4|Lift-and-shift as PaaS`],stem:`Match each scenario to the cloud service type that fits it best. Each service type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Move existing servers to the cloud as they are (lift-and-shift)`,why:`Lift-and-shift keeps your servers and OS as they are: IaaS.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Give developers a framework to build and customize cloud apps`,why:`A development framework is a PaaS scenario.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Provide staff with email and productivity applications`,why:`Ready-made email and productivity apps are SaaS.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Start and stop test environments quickly while keeping complete control`,why:`Testing and development with complete control is an IaaS scenario.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`IaaS`},{id:`t2`,text:`PaaS`},{id:`t3`,text:`SaaS`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t1`},explanation:`IaaS: lift-and-shift and test/dev. PaaS: development frameworks and analytics. SaaS: email, productivity, and finance apps.`},{id:`q3.4-02`,objectiveId:`3.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`3.4|Lift-and-shift as PaaS`],stem:`A company wants to move its existing on-premises servers to Azure quickly, with minimal changes to its applications or operating systems. Which cloud service type fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`PaaS`,why:`PaaS would hand the OS and runtime to the provider, so the servers can’t move as they are.`},{id:`b`,text:`SaaS`,why:`SaaS replaces your applications with finished ones; it doesn’t move your servers.`},{id:`c`,text:`IaaS`,why:`Correct: lift-and-shift migration is Learn’s first IaaS scenario.`},{id:`d`,text:`Serverless computing`,why:`Serverless would mean rewriting the apps as functions.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Lift-and-shift keeps existing servers and operating systems as they are, which is IaaS.`},{id:`q3.4-03`,objectiveId:`3.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A sales team needs a ready-to-use finance and expense tracking application, with no infrastructure or platform to manage. Which cloud service type fits?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)`,why:`IaaS would leave the team managing servers and software.`},{id:`b`,text:`Platform as a service (PaaS)`,why:`PaaS is for building your own app, not using a ready-made one.`},{id:`c`,text:`A hybrid cloud`,why:`Hybrid cloud is a deployment model, not a service type.`},{id:`d`,text:`Software as a service (SaaS)`,why:`Correct: finance and expense tracking is one of Learn’s SaaS scenarios.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Ready-made applications such as finance and expense tracking are SaaS.`},{id:`q3.4-04`,objectiveId:`3.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,stem:`For each statement about choosing a cloud service type, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Analytics and business intelligence tools delivered as a service are a common PaaS scenario.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists analytics and BI as a PaaS scenario.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Test environments you start and stop quickly while keeping complete control are a common SaaS scenario.`,answer:!1,why:`That is a common IaaS scenario.`},{id:`s3`,text:`The more control over the operating system a scenario needs, the closer it is to IaaS.`,answer:!0,why:`IaaS is the only type where you manage the OS, so OS control points to IaaS.`}],explanation:`Use the scenario lists: analytics is PaaS, test/dev with full control is IaaS, and OS control points to IaaS.`},{id:`q3.4-06`,objectiveId:`3.4`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`The less an organization wants to manage itself, the closer the right cloud service type is to ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`IaaS`,why:`IaaS leaves the most for the organization to manage, including the OS.`},{id:`b`,text:`PaaS`,why:`PaaS is the middle ground: you still build and manage your own application.`},{id:`c`,text:`a private cloud`,why:`A private cloud is a deployment model, and it usually means managing more, not less.`},{id:`d`,text:`SaaS`,why:`Correct: with SaaS the provider manages almost everything, and you just use the application.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Moving from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS, the organization manages less each time, so wanting to manage as little as possible points to SaaS.`}],o=[{id:`q4.1-01`,objectiveId:`4.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which statement best describes an Azure region?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A single datacenter that you pick for each resource`,why:`You never choose an individual datacenter, and a region can contain several.`},{id:`b`,text:`One or more nearby datacenters connected by a low-latency network`,why:`Correct: that is Learn’s definition of a region.`},{id:`c`,text:`A logical container that groups resources for billing and access control`,why:`That describes a subscription (billing and access) or a resource group (organizing resources), not a region.`},{id:`d`,text:`An isolated instance of Azure used only for government or legal compliance`,why:`That describes a sovereign region, a special kind of region.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`A region is a geographical area containing one or more datacenters that are close together and networked with low latency.`},{id:`q4.1-02`,objectiveId:`4.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.1|Availability zones`],stem:`A company’s workload must keep running even if a natural disaster takes out the entire Azure region it runs in. What should the company use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Availability zones in the same region`,why:`Zones protect against a datacenter failing inside one region, not the whole region going down.`},{id:`b`,text:`An availability set`,why:`Availability sets protect against hardware failures and maintenance, not a region-wide disaster.`},{id:`c`,text:`A second resource group in the same region`,why:`Resource groups are logical containers; they don’t change where resources physically run.`},{id:`d`,text:`The region’s paired region`,why:`Correct: region pairs protect against events that take out a whole region, such as a natural disaster.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Availability zones cover a datacenter failure within a region; a region pair, at least 300 miles away in the same geography, covers losing the entire region.`},{id:`q4.1-03`,objectiveId:`4.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.1|Every region has a pair`],stem:`For each statement about Azure regions, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Every Azure region is paired with another region.`,answer:!1,why:`Most regions are paired, but some have no pair.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Planned Azure updates roll out to one region of a pair at a time.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says updates are rolled out to paired regions one at a time.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Some Azure services, such as Microsoft Entra ID and Azure DNS, don’t require you to choose a region.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists these as global services that need no region.`}],explanation:`Region pairs are common but not universal. Paired regions receive planned updates one at a time, and a few services are global rather than regional.`},{id:`q4.1-04`,objectiveId:`4.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A US government agency must run its workloads in Azure, but in an instance of Azure that is isolated from the main public Azure for compliance and legal reasons. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure sovereign regions`,why:`Correct: sovereign regions, such as the US Government regions, are isolated instances of Azure for compliance or legal needs.`},{id:`b`,text:`A region and its paired region in the same geography`,why:`Region pairs are part of the main Azure instance; they protect against a region-wide disaster, not for isolation.`},{id:`c`,text:`Availability zones`,why:`Zones are part of a normal public region, not an isolated instance of Azure; they protect against datacenter failure.`},{id:`d`,text:`A dedicated subscription with restricted access`,why:`A subscription is a billing and access boundary inside the same Azure instance, not an isolated instance of Azure.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Sovereign regions (the US Government regions, and the China regions operated by 21Vianet) are isolated from the main instance of Azure.`},{id:`q4.2-01`,objectiveId:`4.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`Every Azure region that supports availability zones has a minimum of ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`two zones`,why:`Learn says the minimum is higher than two.`},{id:`b`,text:`one zone for each datacenter`,why:`The number of zones doesn’t depend on how many datacenters there are; one zone can contain several datacenters.`},{id:`c`,text:`three zones`,why:`Correct: zone-enabled regions have a minimum of three availability zones.`},{id:`d`,text:`four zones`,why:`Learn gives a minimum of three, not four.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Every zone-enabled region has a minimum of three availability zones, though not every region supports zones.`},{id:`q4.2-02`,objectiveId:`4.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.2|Availability sets`],stem:`A company wants its application to keep running if an entire datacenter fails, without moving the application out of its current region. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`An availability set`,why:`Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains to survive a hardware failure or maintenance event, not a whole datacenter failing.`},{id:`b`,text:`Availability zones`,why:`Correct: availability zones are physically separate datacenters within one region, so a copy in another zone keeps running.`},{id:`c`,text:`A region pair`,why:`A region pair uses a different region, and the company wants to stay in its current one.`},{id:`d`,text:`A larger virtual machine size`,why:`A bigger VM still runs in one datacenter, so it doesn’t survive that datacenter failing.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Availability zones are isolation boundaries inside a region: if one zone’s datacenter fails, copies in the other zones keep working.`},{id:`q4.2-03`,objectiveId:`4.2`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each description to the category of Azure service that availability zones use. Each category may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`A virtual machine that you deploy to one specific zone`,why:`You pin it to a zone, so it is a zonal service.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Storage that Azure automatically copies across zones for you`,why:`Azure replicates it across zones, so it is zone-redundant.`},{id:`i3`,text:`A service that is always available, whatever happens to a zone or region`,why:`Learn calls these non-regional services.`},{id:`i4`,text:`A public IP address that you pin to one zone`,why:`IP addresses are one of Learn’s examples of zonal resources.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Zonal service`},{id:`t2`,text:`Zone-redundant service`},{id:`t3`,text:`Non-regional service`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t1`},explanation:`Zonal: you pin the resource to a zone (VMs, managed disks, IP addresses). Zone-redundant: Azure replicates across zones (ZRS, SQL Database). Non-regional: always available.`},{id:`q4.3-01`,objectiveId:`4.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company deploying its first Azure VM wants it to run in a particular datacenter building near its office. Which physical location can it actually choose?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The specific datacenter building to run it in`,why:`You never choose an individual datacenter; Azure groups datacenters for you.`},{id:`b`,text:`A server rack`,why:`Racks and hosts are managed entirely by Microsoft.`},{id:`c`,text:`The fault domain inside the datacenter`,why:`Fault domains are how an availability set spreads VMs; you don’t pick a physical location by them.`},{id:`d`,text:`A region, and optionally a zone`,why:`Correct: you choose a region, and sometimes an availability zone, never a specific building.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Datacenters are the bottom layer of Azure’s physical infrastructure. You work with the regions and availability zones they’re grouped into.`},{id:`q4.3-02`,objectiveId:`4.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure datacenters, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Physical security of datacenter buildings is the outermost layer of the defense-in-depth model.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists physical security as the first layer, from the outside in.`},{id:`s2`,text:`An Azure datacenter has dedicated power, cooling, and networking.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn describes datacenters this way.`},{id:`s3`,text:`For IaaS workloads, the customer is responsible for the physical security of the datacenter.`,answer:!1,why:`The physical datacenter is always Microsoft’s responsibility in the cloud.`}],explanation:`A datacenter is a physical facility with its own power, cooling, and networking. Securing it is always Microsoft’s job, and it’s the outermost layer of defense in depth.`},{id:`q4.3-03`,objectiveId:`4.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.3|Availability zone`],stem:`Azure groups its datacenters into ___, and those are grouped into regions.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`resource groups`,why:`Resource groups are logical containers for resources, not groups of datacenters.`},{id:`b`,text:`availability zones`,why:`Correct: an availability zone is one or more datacenters, and a zone-enabled region has several zones.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure subscriptions`,why:`Subscriptions are billing and access boundaries, not physical groupings.`},{id:`d`,text:`management groups`,why:`Management groups organize subscriptions; they’re part of the management hierarchy, not the physical one.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`The physical hierarchy is datacenters → availability zones → regions. Resource groups, subscriptions, and management groups are the management hierarchy.`},{id:`q4.4-01`,objectiveId:`4.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A developer is building a temporary test environment with several VMs, a database, and a virtual network. When the test ends, everything must be removed in one step. What should the developer do?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Tag each resource, then delete the tag`,why:`Deleting a tag removes the label, not the resources.`},{id:`b`,text:`Put everything in one resource group, then delete the group`,why:`Correct: deleting a resource group deletes every resource inside it.`},{id:`c`,text:`Move the resources into a management group, then delete that group`,why:`Management groups hold subscriptions, not individual resources.`},{id:`d`,text:`Place the VMs in an availability set`,why:`Availability sets improve VM resiliency; they don’t help remove resources.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Resource groups are useful for lifecycle grouping: put a temporary environment in one group and delete the group when you’re done.`},{id:`q4.4-02`,objectiveId:`4.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about resource groups, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A resource can belong to two resource groups at the same time.`,answer:!1,why:`Every resource belongs to exactly one resource group at a time.`},{id:`s2`,text:`You can nest a resource group inside another resource group.`,answer:!1,why:`Resource groups can’t be nested.`},{id:`s3`,text:`You can move some resources from one resource group to another.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says some resources can be moved between groups, though each is in only one group at a time.`}],explanation:`A resource is in exactly one resource group at a time (though some can be moved), and resource groups can’t be nested.`},{id:`q4.4-03`,objectiveId:`4.4`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.4|Subscription`],stem:`Which two statements about resource groups and subscriptions are true? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A subscription can contain several resource groups`,why:`Correct: subscriptions contain resource groups.`},{id:`b`,text:`You can rename a resource group after you create it`,why:`Resource groups can’t be renamed after they’re created.`},{id:`c`,text:`A resource group can contain subscriptions`,why:`It’s the other way around: subscriptions contain resource groups.`},{id:`d`,text:`A subscription is a billing boundary; a resource group organizes the resources inside it`,why:`Correct: that is the difference Learn draws between the two.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`Resource groups organize resources; a subscription is the billing and access boundary that contains resource groups.`},{id:`q4.5-01`,objectiveId:`4.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants separate Azure invoices for its production environment and its development environment. What should it create?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Separate subscriptions`,why:`Correct: a subscription is a billing boundary, and Azure produces separate invoices for each one.`},{id:`b`,text:`Separate resource groups in one subscription`,why:`Resource groups organize resources, but billing is per subscription, so they’d share one invoice.`},{id:`c`,text:`Separate regions`,why:`Regions are physical locations; they don’t split billing.`},{id:`d`,text:`Separate Microsoft Entra user accounts`,why:`Accounts are identities that sign in; the subscriptions linked to them are what get billed.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Subscriptions are billing boundaries. Organizations commonly add subscriptions to split billing, for example between production and dev/test.`},{id:`q4.5-02`,objectiveId:`4.5`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.5|Azure account`],stem:`An Azure account is an identity in ___, and the subscriptions linked to it are what get billed.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Resource Manager`,why:`Resource Manager is the deployment and management service, not the identity directory.`},{id:`b`,text:`a resource group`,why:`A resource group holds resources, not identities.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Azure Policy enforces rules on resources; it doesn’t hold identities.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra ID`,why:`Correct: an Azure account is an identity in Microsoft Entra ID or a directory it trusts.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`The account signs in; subscriptions linked to it provide access to services and are billed.`},{id:`q4.5-03`,objectiveId:`4.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure subscriptions, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`One Azure account can have more than one subscription.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says an account can have several subscriptions.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Subscriptions can be organized into management groups.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says subscriptions can be organized into management groups.`},{id:`s3`,text:`A subscription is a unit of scale only; it has no effect on billing.`,answer:!1,why:`A subscription is a unit of management, billing, and scale.`}],explanation:`A subscription is a unit of management, billing, and scale, and an account can have several.`},{id:`q4.6-01`,objectiveId:`4.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`4.6|Resource groups`],stem:`A company has 40 subscriptions. It wants one Azure Policy that limits VM locations to apply to all of them, assigned once. Where should it assign the policy?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A resource group that contains all 40 subscriptions`,why:`Resource groups hold resources, not subscriptions, and they can’t be nested.`},{id:`b`,text:`Each subscription’s largest resource group`,why:`That means 40 separate assignments, and it wouldn’t cover the other resource groups.`},{id:`c`,text:`A management group above the subscriptions`,why:`Correct: every subscription in a management group inherits the conditions applied to it.`},{id:`d`,text:`The Azure account that owns the subscriptions`,why:`An account is an identity; policies aren’t assigned to it.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Management groups organize subscriptions so a policy or access assignment on the group applies to every subscription in it.`},{id:`q4.6-02`,objectiveId:`4.6`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`Management groups can be nested up to ___ levels deep, not counting the root level or the subscription level.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`six`,why:`Correct: management groups nest up to six levels deep, excluding the root and subscription levels.`},{id:`b`,text:`three`,why:`Three is the minimum number of availability zones in a zone-enabled region, not the nesting limit.`},{id:`c`,text:`ten`,why:`Learn gives six levels, not ten.`},{id:`d`,text:`two`,why:`Management groups can nest deeper than two levels.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`The six-level limit lets the hierarchy mirror the organization, with every group folding up to the Tenant Root Group.`},{id:`q4.6-03`,objectiveId:`4.6`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about management groups, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A management group can have more than one parent.`,answer:!1,why:`Each management group and subscription can have only one parent.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Every Microsoft Entra tenant has a single top-level root management group.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn calls it the Tenant Root Group; all other groups and subscriptions fold up to it.`},{id:`s3`,text:`A management group can hold individual resources directly, without a subscription.`,answer:!1,why:`Management groups hold subscriptions (and other management groups); resources sit in resource groups inside subscriptions.`}],explanation:`Management groups form a tree of management groups and subscriptions under one Tenant Root Group, and each has only one parent.`},{id:`q4.7-01`,objectiveId:`4.7`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each description to the level of Azure’s management hierarchy. Each level may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Organizes subscriptions and can be nested`,why:`Management groups sit above subscriptions and can nest.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Gets its own invoice and is an access control boundary`,why:`Each subscription is billed separately and is an access boundary.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Holds resources directly, and can’t be renamed after it’s created`,why:`Resource groups hold resources directly (subscriptions hold resource groups), and they can’t be renamed.`},{id:`i4`,text:`A virtual machine, a virtual network, or a database`,why:`These are resources, the bottom of the hierarchy.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Management group`},{id:`t2`,text:`Subscription`},{id:`t3`,text:`Resource group`},{id:`t4`,text:`Resource`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Top to bottom: management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources.`},{id:`q4.7-02`,objectiveId:`4.7`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A policy assigned to the Production management group allows VMs in only one region. A subscription owner under that group tries to create a VM in a different region. What happens?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The VM is created, because the owner has full control of the subscription`,why:`A subscription owner can’t override a policy inherited from a management group.`},{id:`b`,text:`The VM is created in the allowed region instead`,why:`Azure doesn’t move the request to another region; the inherited policy blocks it.`},{id:`c`,text:`The VM creation is blocked by the inherited policy`,why:`Correct: settings flow downward, and a policy inherited from above can’t be overridden by a subscription owner.`},{id:`d`,text:`The VM is created, but only in the subscription’s default resource group`,why:`Resource groups don’t change the location rule; the inherited policy still applies.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Policies applied to a management group are inherited by every subscription, resource group, and resource beneath it, and owners below can’t override them.`},{id:`q4.7-03`,objectiveId:`4.7`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`4.7|Physical hierarchy`],stem:`Which two are levels of Azure’s management hierarchy, the structure used to organize and govern resources? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Availability zones`,why:`Zones are part of the physical infrastructure: where resources run, not how they’re organized.`},{id:`b`,text:`Subscriptions`,why:`Correct: subscriptions sit between management groups and resource groups.`},{id:`c`,text:`Resource groups`,why:`Correct: resource groups sit between subscriptions and resources.`},{id:`d`,text:`Datacenters`,why:`Datacenters are the bottom layer of the physical infrastructure.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`Management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources is how you organize and govern; datacenters, zones, and regions are where resources physically run.`},{id:`q4.7-04`,objectiveId:`4.7`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`An administrator assigns a role on a resource group. Where does that role assignment apply?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Only to the resource group itself, not the resources in it`,why:`Resources inherit settings from their resource group.`},{id:`b`,text:`To every resource group in the same subscription`,why:`Inheritance flows down, not sideways to other resource groups.`},{id:`c`,text:`To the parent subscription`,why:`Inheritance flows down the hierarchy, not up.`},{id:`d`,text:`To every resource in that resource group`,why:`Correct: role assignments are inherited by everything below the scope where they’re applied.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Settings such as policies and role assignments flow downward in the hierarchy: resources inherit from their resource group.`}],s=[{id:`q5.1-01`,objectiveId:`5.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.1|Containers vs VMs`],stem:`A company is splitting an app into microservices that run continuously. Each part must scale and update on its own, start quickly, and share a host with other parts, and the team doesn’t want to manage an operating system for each part. Which compute type fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A virtual machine for each part`,why:`Each VM runs its own operating system that you manage, which the team wants to avoid.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Virtual Desktop`,why:`Azure Virtual Desktop delivers Windows desktops and apps to users; it doesn’t host microservices.`},{id:`c`,text:`Containers`,why:`Correct: many containers run on one host, you don’t manage their OS, and they start and scale quickly, which suits microservices.`},{id:`d`,text:`A single larger virtual machine`,why:`One VM can’t scale or update each part independently, and you still manage its OS.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Containers are lightweight: many run on a shared host without you managing their operating system, so each microservice can scale or update independently.`},{id:`q5.1-02`,objectiveId:`5.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure compute types, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Each virtual machine runs its own operating system.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says each VM runs its own OS, which you manage.`},{id:`s2`,text:`With containers, you manage the operating system inside each container.`,answer:!1,why:`Containers run on a shared host, and you don’t manage their operating system.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Moving from VMs to containers to functions, you manage less and control less.`,answer:!0,why:`That is the trade-off Learn describes across the three compute types.`}],explanation:`VMs give the most control (and the most to manage); containers and then functions hand more of the environment to Azure.`},{id:`q5.1-03`,objectiveId:`5.1`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each requirement to the compute type that fits it best. Each compute type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Install custom software and keep total control of the operating system`,why:`Total OS control and custom software point to virtual machines (IaaS).`},{id:`i2`,text:`Run a short task only when a new file arrives, and pay only while it runs`,why:`Event-driven code that runs only when triggered is serverless: Azure Functions.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Run many isolated app components on one host and scale each one quickly`,why:`Many lightweight units per host that scale quickly are containers.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Move an existing server to Azure as it is (lift-and-shift)`,why:`Lift-and-shift keeps the server and its OS, so it needs a VM.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Virtual machines`},{id:`t2`,text:`Containers`},{id:`t3`,text:`Azure Functions`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t3`,i3:`t2`,i4:`t1`},explanation:`VMs: OS control, custom software, lift-and-shift. Containers: microservices on a shared host. Functions: short, event-driven tasks.`},{id:`q5.1-04`,objectiveId:`5.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company runs hundreds of containers across many hosts and needs a service to orchestrate them. Which Azure service should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)`,why:`Correct: AKS is Azure’s container orchestration service.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Container Instances`,why:`Container Instances is the simplest way to run a container, without orchestration.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets`,why:`Scale sets manage groups of identical VMs, not containers.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Functions`,why:`Functions runs event-driven code, not containers at scale.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Container Instances is the simplest PaaS option, Container Apps adds built-in load balancing and scaling, and AKS provides orchestration.`},{id:`q5.2-01`,objectiveId:`5.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.2|Scale sets vs availability sets`],stem:`A company’s web tier runs on identical VMs. It wants Azure to add VMs automatically during busy periods and remove them when demand drops, all from one central configuration. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`An availability set`,why:`Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains; they don’t change how many VMs run.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets`,why:`Correct: scale sets manage identical, load-balanced VMs and scale out or in automatically with demand.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Virtual Desktop`,why:`Azure Virtual Desktop hosts desktops and apps for users, not an automatically scaling web tier.`},{id:`d`,text:`A region pair for the web tier`,why:`Region pairs protect against a region-wide disaster; they don’t add VMs when demand rises.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Scale sets change how many VMs run to meet demand; availability sets spread VMs so one failure doesn’t take them all down.`},{id:`q5.2-02`,objectiveId:`5.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which option spreads VMs across fault domains and update domains, so that a hardware failure or a planned maintenance event doesn’t take them all down at once?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A region pair`,why:`Region pairs protect against losing a whole region, not by spreading VMs across fault and update domains.`},{id:`b`,text:`A resource group`,why:`A resource group organizes resources; it doesn’t affect where VMs physically run.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Virtual Desktop`,why:`Azure Virtual Desktop delivers desktops and apps; fault and update domains are what availability sets use.`},{id:`d`,text:`An availability set`,why:`Correct: availability sets spread VMs across update domains and fault domains.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Update domains are rebooted together during planned maintenance; fault domains share a power or network failure point. Spreading VMs across both is what an availability set does, at no extra cost.`},{id:`q5.2-03`,objectiveId:`5.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`___ lets users securely access Windows desktops and apps from many devices, with single-session or multi-session options.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets`,why:`Scale sets run groups of identical VMs that scale with demand; they don’t deliver desktops to users.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Virtual Desktop`,why:`Correct: Azure Virtual Desktop is a managed desktop and app virtualization service.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure App Service`,why:`App Service hosts web apps and APIs, not Windows desktops.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,why:`Domain Services provides managed domain join and legacy authentication, not desktops.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure Virtual Desktop provides cloud-hosted Windows desktops and apps, single- or multi-session, with Microsoft Entra ID for access.`},{id:`q5.2-04`,objectiveId:`5.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`5.2|What each option protects against`],stem:`For each statement about VM resiliency options, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`You pay extra for an availability set, on top of the VMs in it.`,answer:!1,why:`Availability sets cost nothing extra; you pay only for the VM instances.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Availability zones protect against a datacenter failure, and a region pair protects against a region-wide disaster.`,answer:!0,why:`Zones are separate datacenters in one region; a region pair covers losing the whole region.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Running several load-balanced VM instances in a scale set can improve availability as well as scale.`,answer:!0,why:`Scale sets are mainly for scaling, but several load-balanced instances also improve availability.`}],explanation:`Availability set: hardware or maintenance failure. Availability zone: datacenter failure. Region pair: region-wide disaster. Scale set: mainly scaling, with availability as a side benefit.`},{id:`q5.3-01`,objectiveId:`5.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two are resources you choose when you create an Azure virtual machine? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A size, which sets the processor cores and RAM`,why:`Correct: the VM size sets its purpose, processor cores, and RAM.`},{id:`b`,text:`A management group`,why:`Management groups organize subscriptions; they aren’t a resource a VM needs.`},{id:`c`,text:`An App Service plan`,why:`App Service plans are for App Service apps, not VMs.`},{id:`d`,text:`Networking, such as a virtual network and a public IP address`,why:`Correct: a VM needs a virtual network, a public IP address, and port configuration.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`A VM needs a size, storage disks, and networking (virtual network, public IP address, ports).`},{id:`q5.3-02`,objectiveId:`5.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.3|Azure Disks vs Blob storage`],stem:`A company is creating a VM and needs block-level storage volumes attached to it for the operating system and application data. Which Azure Storage service provides these?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Blob Storage`,why:`Blob storage is object storage reached over HTTP or HTTPS, not block-level volumes for a VM.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Files`,why:`Azure Files provides file shares mounted over SMB or NFS, not a VM’s block-level disks.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Disks`,why:`Correct: Azure Disks provides block-level storage volumes for Azure VMs.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Table Storage`,why:`Table Storage is a NoSQL store for structured data.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`A VM’s OS and data disks are Azure Disks: block-level volumes. Blob storage is object storage accessed by URL or API.`},{id:`q5.3-03`,objectiveId:`5.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`VMs are usually deployed from ___: a template that already includes an operating system and tools.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`an image`,why:`Correct: an image is a template that includes an operating system and tools.`},{id:`b`,text:`a size`,why:`A size sets the processor cores and RAM, not the operating system.`},{id:`c`,text:`a resource group`,why:`A resource group is where the VM is organized, not what it’s built from.`},{id:`d`,text:`an availability set`,why:`An availability set spreads VMs across fault and update domains; it doesn’t contain an OS.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Images are templates with an OS and tools already in them; you pick the size, disks, and networking separately.`},{id:`q5.4-01`,objectiveId:`5.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A team wants to deploy its Python web app code and have Azure run the environment, including automatic scaling and load balancing. It doesn’t want to manage servers, operating systems, or containers. Which should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure VMs`,why:`VMs give maximum control, but the team would manage the servers and OS.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Kubernetes Service`,why:`AKS orchestrates containers, which the team doesn’t want to manage.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Virtual Desktop`,why:`Azure Virtual Desktop delivers desktops and apps to users; it doesn’t host web apps.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure App Service`,why:`Correct: App Service hosts web apps in languages including Python, with built-in scaling and load balancing and no infrastructure to manage.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`App Service is the platform option for web apps: deploy your code and let Azure run the environment.`},{id:`q5.4-02`,objectiveId:`5.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`5.4|App Service vs Azure Functions`],stem:`For each statement about Azure App Service, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`App Service can host API apps and mobile app back ends, not only websites.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists web apps, API apps, WebJobs, and mobile app back ends.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Like Azure Functions, App Service runs your code only when an event triggers it.`,answer:!1,why:`App Service keeps a web app or API available to serve requests; Functions runs code only when triggered.`},{id:`s3`,text:`App Service runs apps only on Windows.`,answer:!1,why:`App Service runs on Windows or Linux.`}],explanation:`App Service is an HTTP-based hosting platform that keeps your app available; Azure Functions is event-driven serverless code.`},{id:`q5.4-03`,objectiveId:`5.4`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each hosting need to the option that fits it best. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Maximum control of the hosting environment`,why:`Learn says VMs give you maximum control of the hosting environment.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Isolate and manage different parts of the solution individually`,why:`Learn gives this as the reason to choose containers.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Deploy web code and let Azure run the environment`,why:`That is App Service, the platform option.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Virtual machines`},{id:`t2`,text:`Containers`},{id:`t3`,text:`Azure App Service`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`},explanation:`VMs for full control, containers for independently managed components, App Service when you just want to deploy web code.`},{id:`q5.5-01`,objectiveId:`5.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.5|VPN Gateway vs ExpressRoute`],stem:`A company needs a connection between its on-premises network and Azure that doesn’t travel over the public internet and offers consistent latency. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure VPN Gateway with a site-to-site VPN`,why:`A VPN gateway encrypts traffic, but it still travels over the public internet.`},{id:`b`,text:`Virtual network peering`,why:`Peering connects Azure virtual networks to each other, not to on-premises.`},{id:`c`,text:`ExpressRoute`,why:`Correct: ExpressRoute is a private connection through a connectivity provider that doesn’t use the public internet.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure DNS`,why:`Azure DNS provides name resolution, not a network connection.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`VPN Gateway sends encrypted traffic across the internet; ExpressRoute bypasses the internet for more reliability, faster speeds, and consistent latency.`},{id:`q5.5-02`,objectiveId:`5.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.5|VNet peering vs VPN`],stem:`A company has two Azure virtual networks in different regions. Resources in them must communicate privately, with traffic staying on the Microsoft backbone network. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A shared subnet`,why:`Subnets divide one virtual network; they can’t span two networks.`},{id:`b`,text:`Virtual network peering`,why:`Correct: peering connects two VNets directly, even across regions, over the Microsoft backbone.`},{id:`c`,text:`A point-to-site VPN`,why:`Point-to-site connects a single computer to a VNet over the internet.`},{id:`d`,text:`ExpressRoute`,why:`ExpressRoute connects an on-premises network to Azure, not two Azure VNets to each other.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Peering links Azure VNets directly over the Microsoft backbone; a VPN encrypts traffic across the internet, usually to on-premises.`},{id:`q5.5-03`,objectiveId:`5.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure networking, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`You can divide a virtual network’s address space into subnets.`,answer:!0,why:`Subnets divide a VNet for isolation and segmentation.`},{id:`s2`,text:`You can buy a new domain name through Azure DNS.`,answer:!1,why:`Azure DNS hosts domains and records, but you can’t buy a domain with it.`},{id:`s3`,text:`A single virtual network can have several VPN gateways.`,answer:!1,why:`Each virtual network can have only one VPN gateway.`}],explanation:`VNets are divided into subnets; Azure DNS hosts domains but doesn’t sell them; each VNet can have only one VPN gateway.`},{id:`q5.5-04`,objectiveId:`5.5`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`A point-to-site VPN connects ___ to an Azure virtual network.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`an entire on-premises network`,why:`That is a site-to-site VPN.`},{id:`b`,text:`two VNets`,why:`Connecting two VNets is VNet-to-VNet (or peering), not point-to-site.`},{id:`c`,text:`a connectivity provider’s private circuit`,why:`A connectivity provider’s private connection is ExpressRoute.`},{id:`d`,text:`a single computer`,why:`Correct: point-to-site connects one computer; site-to-site connects a whole network.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`VPN Gateway supports site-to-site (a whole network), point-to-site (one computer), and VNet-to-VNet connections.`},{id:`q5.6-01`,objectiveId:`5.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`5.6|Service endpoints`],stem:`Which gives an Azure service a private IP address from your virtual network’s own address space?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A private endpoint`,why:`Correct: a private endpoint has a private IP address from the VNet’s address space, bringing the service into your VNet.`},{id:`b`,text:`A service endpoint`,why:`Service endpoints link services like Azure SQL and Storage to a VNet, without giving the service its own private IP in it.`},{id:`c`,text:`A public endpoint`,why:`A public endpoint has a public IP address reachable from anywhere.`},{id:`d`,text:`A public load balancer`,why:`A public load balancer makes resources reachable from the internet.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`A private endpoint brings the service into your virtual network with its own private IP address; a service endpoint links the service to the VNet.`},{id:`q5.6-02`,objectiveId:`5.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to allow only HTTPS traffic into a subnet and block everything else, based on source and destination IP address, port, and protocol. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure DNS`,why:`Azure DNS resolves names; it doesn’t filter traffic.`},{id:`b`,text:`Virtual network peering`,why:`Peering connects VNets; it doesn’t allow or block traffic by port.`},{id:`c`,text:`A network security group`,why:`Correct: network security groups allow or block traffic by source and destination IP address, port, and protocol.`},{id:`d`,text:`A private endpoint for the subnet`,why:`A private endpoint gives a service a private IP address; it doesn’t filter traffic by port.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Network security groups are Azure’s rule-based traffic filters: allow or deny by IP address, port, and protocol.`},{id:`q5.6-03`,objectiveId:`5.6`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two make an Azure resource reachable from the internet? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Giving it a private endpoint`,why:`A private endpoint uses a private IP address inside your VNet.`},{id:`b`,text:`Assigning it a public IP address`,why:`Correct: a public IP address makes a resource reachable from the internet.`},{id:`c`,text:`Placing it behind a public load balancer`,why:`Correct: Learn names a public load balancer as the other way to expose a resource.`},{id:`d`,text:`Connecting its VNet to another VNet with peering`,why:`Peering is private traffic over the Microsoft backbone.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`You make a resource internet-reachable with a public IP address or a public load balancer; private endpoints and peering keep traffic private.`}],c=[{id:`q6.1-01`,objectiveId:`6.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`6.1|Blobs vs Files`],stem:`A company needs a file share that its on-premises servers and its Azure VMs can mount at the same time over SMB, like a network drive. Which Azure Storage service should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Blob Storage`,why:`Blobs are objects reached by URL or API, not a share you mount like a network drive.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Files`,why:`Correct: Azure Files offers managed file shares that cloud or on-premises machines can mount at the same time over SMB or NFS.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Disks`,why:`Azure Disks are block-level volumes attached to Azure VMs, not shares for on-premises servers.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Queue Storage`,why:`Queues hold messages between app components, not files.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure Files is for shares you mount over SMB or NFS; Blob storage is object storage reached by URL or API.`},{id:`q6.1-02`,objectiveId:`6.1`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each kind of data to the Azure Storage service that fits it best. Each service may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Images and videos served directly to a browser`,why:`Unstructured objects such as media are Blob storage.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Messages passed between app components for asynchronous processing`,why:`That is what Azure Queues is for.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Large amounts of structured, non-relational data`,why:`Azure Tables is a NoSQL store for structured, non-relational data.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Backups and archives kept for the long term`,why:`Learn lists backups and archives as Blob storage use cases.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Azure Blobs`},{id:`t2`,text:`Azure Files`},{id:`t3`,text:`Azure Queues`},{id:`t4`,text:`Azure Tables`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t3`,i3:`t4`,i4:`t1`},explanation:`Blobs: unstructured objects (media, backups, archives). Queues: messages. Tables: NoSQL structured data. Files: mounted shares.`},{id:`q6.1-03`,objectiveId:`6.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.1|Tables vs a relational database`],stem:`For each statement about Azure Storage services, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure Tables is a relational SQL database.`,answer:!1,why:`Azure Tables is a NoSQL store for structured, non-relational data.`},{id:`s2`,text:`All the core Azure Storage services can be accessed over HTTP or HTTPS.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says every service is accessible over HTTP or HTTPS.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Data in Azure Storage is stored unencrypted unless you add your own encryption.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says data is encrypted by the service.`}],explanation:`The storage services share their benefits (durable, encrypted, scalable, managed, reachable over HTTP/HTTPS), but Tables is NoSQL, not relational.`},{id:`q6.1-04`,objectiveId:`6.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`Each message in Azure Queue Storage can be up to ___ in size.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`8 KB`,why:`Learn gives a larger maximum message size than 8 KB.`},{id:`b`,text:`256 KB`,why:`Learn gives 64 KB as the maximum for Queue Storage messages.`},{id:`c`,text:`1 GB`,why:`Queue messages are small; 1 GB is far above the limit.`},{id:`d`,text:`64 KB`,why:`Correct: each Queue Storage message can be up to 64 KB.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Queue Storage holds large numbers of small messages, each up to 64 KB, for asynchronous processing between components.`},{id:`q6.2-01`,objectiveId:`6.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company stores product images that its website loads many times a day. Which blob access tier should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Hot`,why:`Correct: the hot tier is for data accessed frequently, such as website images.`},{id:`b`,text:`Cool`,why:`The cool tier is for infrequently accessed data, with higher access costs.`},{id:`c`,text:`Archive`,why:`Archive is offline: the images couldn’t be read until they were rehydrated.`},{id:`d`,text:`Zone-redundant`,why:`Zone-redundant storage is a redundancy option, not an access tier.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Hot is for frequent access. Cooler tiers cost less to store but more to access, so they’d cost more for data read constantly.`},{id:`q6.2-02`,objectiveId:`6.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`A company keeps compliance records that are read infrequently and are always stored for at least 90 days. When someone does need a record, it must be readable right away. Which access tier gives the lowest storage cost that meets these needs?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Hot`,why:`Hot is for frequent access and has the highest storage cost.`},{id:`b`,text:`Cool`,why:`Cool is for data stored at least 30 days; for data kept 90 days or more, cold costs less to store.`},{id:`c`,text:`Cold`,why:`Correct: cold is for infrequently accessed data stored at least 90 days, and it’s online, so records can be read right away.`},{id:`d`,text:`Archive`,why:`Archive is cheapest to store but offline; records would have to be rehydrated first, which can take hours.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Cool (at least 30 days) and cold (at least 90 days) are both online tiers for infrequent access; for data kept 90+ days, cold has the lower storage cost. Archive (at least 180 days) is offline.`},{id:`q6.2-03`,objectiveId:`6.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.2|Cool and cold vs archive`],stem:`For each statement about blob access tiers, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Cooler tiers cost less to store data but more to access it.`,answer:!0,why:`That is the trade-off Learn describes across the tiers.`},{id:`s2`,text:`You can set archive as a storage account’s default access tier.`,answer:!1,why:`Hot, cool, and cold can be the account default; archive can be set only on individual blobs.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Data in the cool tier is online, so you can read it at any time.`,answer:!0,why:`Cool and cold are online tiers; only archive is offline.`}],explanation:`Cool and cold stay online at a higher access cost; archive is offline and is set only on individual blobs.`},{id:`q6.2-04`,objectiveId:`6.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.2|Cool and cold vs archive`],stem:`Before you can read a blob in the archive tier, it must be ___ to the hot, cool, or cold tier.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`failed over`,why:`Failover switches to a secondary region; it doesn’t bring a blob out of archive.`},{id:`b`,text:`rehydrated`,why:`Correct: archived blobs are offline until you rehydrate them to an online tier.`},{id:`c`,text:`geo-replicated`,why:`Geo-replication copies data to another region; the copy would still be archived.`},{id:`d`,text:`locked`,why:`A resource lock prevents changes or deletion; it doesn’t bring a blob back online.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Archive is offline: an archived blob can’t be read or modified until it’s rehydrated to an online tier.`},{id:`q6.3-01`,objectiveId:`6.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.3|ZRS vs GRS`],stem:`A company’s data must stay readable and writable if one availability zone fails. All copies must stay in the primary region. Which redundancy option should it choose?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Locally redundant storage (LRS)`,why:`LRS keeps all three copies in one datacenter, so a zone failure could take them all out.`},{id:`b`,text:`Geo-redundant storage (GRS)`,why:`GRS copies data to a secondary region, which the company doesn’t want, and its primary copies are LRS.`},{id:`c`,text:`Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS)`,why:`GZRS also copies data to a secondary region, which the company doesn’t want.`},{id:`d`,text:`Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)`,why:`Correct: ZRS copies data synchronously across three availability zones in the primary region.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`ZRS protects against a zone failure within one region; GRS and GZRS add a secondary region to protect against losing the whole primary region.`},{id:`q6.3-02`,objectiveId:`6.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.3|GRS vs RA-GRS`],stem:`A company wants its data copied to a secondary region, and it wants to read from that secondary copy at any time, without waiting for a failover. Which option should it choose?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`RA-GRS`,why:`Correct: read-access geo-redundant storage lets you read from the secondary region at any time.`},{id:`b`,text:`GRS`,why:`With GRS, the secondary copy is usable only after a failover.`},{id:`c`,text:`ZRS`,why:`ZRS keeps all copies in the primary region.`},{id:`d`,text:`LRS`,why:`LRS keeps all copies in one datacenter in the primary region.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`GRS and RA-GRS both copy data to a secondary region; only the read-access variant lets you read it before a failover (the data may lag behind the primary).`},{id:`q6.3-03`,objectiveId:`6.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company’s data must survive an availability zone failure in the primary region, and also an outage of the entire primary region. It doesn’t need to read the secondary copy before a failover. Which redundancy option meets these needs?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`ZRS`,why:`ZRS survives a zone failure but keeps everything in the primary region, so it doesn’t survive a region outage.`},{id:`b`,text:`GRS`,why:`GRS uses LRS in the primary region: all three primary copies are in one datacenter, so a zone failure can take them out.`},{id:`c`,text:`GZRS`,why:`Correct: GZRS uses ZRS in the primary region plus copies in the secondary region.`},{id:`d`,text:`LRS`,why:`LRS survives neither a zone failure nor a region outage.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`GZRS = ZRS in the primary region + copies in the secondary region. GRS also reaches a secondary region, but its primary copies sit in a single datacenter.`},{id:`q6.3-04`,objectiveId:`6.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`For each statement about Azure Storage redundancy, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure Storage always keeps three copies of your data in the primary region.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says data is always stored three times in the primary region.`},{id:`s2`,text:`LRS protects your data if the entire datacenter is destroyed.`,answer:!1,why:`LRS keeps all three copies in one datacenter, so it doesn’t protect against a datacenter disaster.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Replication to the secondary region happens asynchronously.`,answer:!0,why:`Geo-replication to the secondary region is asynchronous, so the secondary may lag behind.`}],explanation:`Every option keeps three copies in the primary region; LRS keeps them in one datacenter, ZRS across zones, and the geo options also copy asynchronously to a secondary region.`},{id:`q6.4-01`,objectiveId:`6.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which storage account type does Microsoft recommend for most scenarios?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Premium page blobs`,why:`Premium page blobs support only page blobs and LRS.`},{id:`b`,text:`Premium block blobs`,why:`Premium block blobs are for high transaction rates or low latency, with fewer redundancy options.`},{id:`c`,text:`Standard general-purpose v2`,why:`Correct: it supports blobs, files, queues, and tables with every redundancy option, and Microsoft recommends it for most scenarios.`},{id:`d`,text:`Premium file shares`,why:`Premium file shares are for high-performance file shares, with LRS or ZRS only.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Standard general-purpose v2 is the flexible default; the premium types trade flexibility for performance.`},{id:`q6.4-02`,objectiveId:`6.4`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`Which two are valid names for a new Azure storage account? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`contosodata01`,why:`Correct: lowercase letters and numbers only, and within 3–24 characters.`},{id:`b`,text:`Contoso-Data`,why:`Storage account names can’t contain uppercase letters or hyphens.`},{id:`c`,text:`salesarchive2026`,why:`Correct: lowercase letters and numbers only, and within 3–24 characters.`},{id:`d`,text:`cd`,why:`Names must be at least 3 characters long.`}],answer:[`a`,`c`],explanation:`Account names must be globally unique, 3–24 characters, and use only numbers and lowercase letters, because the name forms each service’s endpoint address.`},{id:`q6.4-03`,objectiveId:`6.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`6.4|Storage account vs storage service`],stem:`For each statement about storage accounts, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Blob Storage, Azure Files, Queue Storage, and Table Storage are services inside a storage account.`,answer:!0,why:`The storage account is the container and namespace; the services live inside it.`},{id:`s2`,text:`A storage account’s name only needs to be unique within its resource group.`,answer:!1,why:`The name forms each service’s endpoint address, so it must be unique across all of Azure.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Premium page blob accounts support every redundancy option.`,answer:!1,why:`Premium page blobs support only LRS.`}],explanation:`A storage account gives your data a unique namespace, and the account type decides which services and redundancy options you get.`},{id:`q6.5-01`,objectiveId:`6.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.5|AzCopy sync vs Azure File Sync`],stem:`A company wants its on-premises Windows file server to stay in sync with Azure Files in both directions, keeping frequently used files on the local server and the rest in the cloud. Which tool should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Storage Explorer`,why:`Storage Explorer is a graphical app for managing files and blobs; it doesn’t keep a server in sync.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure File Sync`,why:`Correct: Azure File Sync keeps a Windows Server and Azure Files in two-way sync, with cloud tiering.`},{id:`c`,text:`AzCopy`,why:`AzCopy syncs in one direction only, from a source to a destination.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Data Box ships data physically for a bulk transfer; it isn’t ongoing sync.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure File Sync makes the local Windows Server act like a cache of the cloud file share, syncing both ways with cloud tiering.`},{id:`q6.5-02`,objectiveId:`6.5`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`When AzCopy synchronizes files, it copies ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`in both directions between two locations`,why:`Two-way sync is Azure File Sync, not AzCopy.`},{id:`b`,text:`whichever copy of each file changed most recently`,why:`AzCopy copies from the source you name to the destination you name.`},{id:`c`,text:`only from Azure to an on-premises server`,why:`AzCopy can copy in whichever direction you name, including uploads to Azure.`},{id:`d`,text:`in one direction, from source to destination`,why:`Correct: AzCopy sync works in one direction only.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`With AzCopy you name a source and a destination, and it copies in that direction only.`},{id:`q6.5-03`,objectiveId:`6.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about tools for moving files, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure Storage Explorer is a graphical app that uses AzCopy behind the scenes.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says Storage Explorer uses AzCopy to handle file and blob operations.`},{id:`s2`,text:`AzCopy is a graphical app for browsing and managing blobs.`,answer:!1,why:`AzCopy is a command-line utility; Storage Explorer is the graphical app.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Azure File Sync is installed on a Linux server.`,answer:!1,why:`Azure File Sync is installed on a Windows Server.`}],explanation:`AzCopy is the command-line tool, Storage Explorer is the graphical app on top of it, and File Sync keeps a Windows Server in sync with Azure Files.`},{id:`q6.6-01`,objectiveId:`6.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`6.6|Azure Migrate vs Azure Data Box`],stem:`A company must move tens of terabytes of archived data to Azure once. Its internet bandwidth is limited, so an upload would take months. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Migrate`,why:`Azure Migrate assesses and migrates workloads over the network, which is the problem here.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure File Sync`,why:`File Sync is ongoing two-way sync over the network, not a one-time bulk move.`},{id:`c`,text:`AzCopy`,why:`AzCopy copies over the network, which would still take months.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Correct: Data Box is a physical device shipped to you, so bulk data moves without the network.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Azure Data Box suits very large data sets when bandwidth is limited: you copy data onto a rugged device, and a carrier returns it for upload.`},{id:`q6.6-02`,objectiveId:`6.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which Azure service is a central hub for discovering, assessing, and migrating on-premises servers, databases, and web apps to Azure?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Migrate`,why:`Correct: Azure Migrate gives one portal to start, run, and track a migration, with integrated assessment and migration tools.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Data Box moves data physically; it doesn’t assess servers or apps.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Azure Arc manages resources outside Azure from Azure; it isn’t a migration hub.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Storage Explorer`,why:`Storage Explorer manages files and blobs in storage accounts.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Azure Migrate covers discovery and assessment plus server, database, and web app migration, and it works with ISV tools too.`},{id:`q6.6-04`,objectiveId:`6.6`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Besides importing data into Azure, Azure Data Box can ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`keep a file server in two-way sync with Azure Files`,why:`Ongoing two-way sync is Azure File Sync.`},{id:`b`,text:`export data out of Azure`,why:`Correct: Data Box also works in reverse, to export data from Azure.`},{id:`c`,text:`assess on-premises servers before a migration`,why:`Discovery and assessment are Azure Migrate’s job.`},{id:`d`,text:`stream data continuously over a private connection`,why:`A private, continuous connection to Azure is ExpressRoute; Data Box is a device you ship.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Data Box moves bulk data offline in either direction: import into Azure, or export from Azure.`}],l=[{id:`q7.1-01`,objectiveId:`7.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`7.1|Entra ID vs Entra Domain Services vs AD DS`],stem:`A company is moving a legacy app to Azure. The app needs domain join, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM authentication, and the company doesn’t want to deploy or maintain domain controllers. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Microsoft Entra ID on its own`,why:`Entra ID is cloud identity for modern apps; on its own it doesn’t provide domain join, LDAP, or Kerberos/NTLM.`},{id:`b`,text:`Active Directory Domain Services on Azure VMs`,why:`That would mean deploying and maintaining the domain controllers yourself.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,why:`Correct: Domain Services provides managed domain join, group policy, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM without you running domain controllers.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra Connect`,why:`Entra Connect syncs identities between on-premises AD and Entra ID; it doesn’t provide domain services.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Entra Domain Services is for legacy apps that can’t use modern authentication: Azure runs the domain controllers for you.`},{id:`q7.1-02`,objectiveId:`7.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants its on-premises Active Directory users to have the same identity in Microsoft Entra ID, kept in sync. Which should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Microsoft Entra Connect`,why:`Correct: Entra Connect synchronizes user identities between on-premises Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID.`},{id:`b`,text:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,why:`Domain Services provides a managed domain for legacy apps; it doesn’t sync your on-premises AD to Entra ID.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure File Sync`,why:`Azure File Sync syncs file servers with Azure Files, not identities.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra Conditional Access`,why:`Conditional Access decides whether sign-ins are allowed; it doesn’t sync identities.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Entra Connect gives users one consistent identity across on-premises and the cloud, and connecting them lets Microsoft detect suspicious sign-ins.`},{id:`q7.1-03`,objectiveId:`7.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about directory services, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Microsoft Entra ID provides single sign-on, application management, and device management.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists these among Entra ID’s capabilities.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Objects you create in a Microsoft Entra Domain Services managed domain sync back to Microsoft Entra ID.`,answer:!1,why:`Sync from Entra ID to Domain Services is one-way, so objects created in the managed domain don’t sync back.`},{id:`s3`,text:`With on-premises AD DS, you maintain the domain controllers yourself.`,answer:!0,why:`On-premises AD DS runs on domain controllers you maintain.`}],explanation:`Entra ID is cloud identity, Entra Domain Services is a managed domain (one-way sync from Entra ID), and AD DS is the directory you run yourself.`},{id:`q7.2-01`,objectiveId:`7.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants employees to sign in once and then open several trusted applications without signing in again. Which should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Multifactor authentication`,why:`MFA asks for an extra factor at sign-in; it doesn’t remove the need to sign in to each app.`},{id:`b`,text:`Single sign-on (SSO)`,why:`Correct: SSO lets a user sign in once and access multiple trusted applications.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure RBAC`,why:`RBAC decides what an identity can do to Azure resources, not how often it signs in.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,why:`Domain Services provides a managed domain for legacy apps; it isn’t the sign-once feature.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`SSO reduces the number of passwords and makes access easier to remove, but it’s only as secure as the initial authenticator.`},{id:`q7.2-02`,objectiveId:`7.2`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each authentication factor to its category. Each category may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`A password`,why:`A password is something you know.`},{id:`i2`,text:`A code sent to your phone`,why:`The phone is something you have.`},{id:`i3`,text:`A fingerprint scan`,why:`A fingerprint is something you are.`},{id:`i4`,text:`A face scan`,why:`Your face is something you are.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Something you know`},{id:`t2`,text:`Something you have`},{id:`t3`,text:`Something you are`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t3`},explanation:`MFA factors come from three categories: something you know, something you have, and something you are.`},{id:`q7.2-03`,objectiveId:`7.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`7.2|Authentication vs authorization`],stem:`Which two statements about authentication and authorization are true? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Authentication establishes who you are`,why:`Correct: authentication answers “who are you?” by requiring credentials.`},{id:`b`,text:`Authentication decides which Azure resources you can change`,why:`That is authorization, for example through Azure RBAC.`},{id:`c`,text:`Authorization decides what an identity is allowed to do`,why:`Correct: authorization decides what an authenticated identity can do.`},{id:`d`,text:`MFA is a form of authorization`,why:`MFA strengthens authentication by asking for an extra factor.`}],answer:[`a`,`c`],explanation:`Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you’re allowed to do.`},{id:`q7.2-04`,objectiveId:`7.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Windows Hello for Business, the Microsoft Authenticator app, and FIDO2 security keys are all ___ options in Microsoft Entra ID.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Conditional Access`,why:`Conditional Access decides whether to allow a sign-in; these are ways to sign in.`},{id:`b`,text:`SSO`,why:`SSO means signing in once for many apps; these are specific sign-in methods without a password.`},{id:`c`,text:`B2B collaboration`,why:`B2B collaboration is for external users, not a sign-in method.`},{id:`d`,text:`passwordless`,why:`Correct: these are the three passwordless options Microsoft Entra ID supports.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Passwordless replaces the password with a trusted device plus a biometric or PIN, giving high security and high convenience.`},{id:`q7.3-01`,objectiveId:`7.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`7.3|B2B collaboration vs B2B direct connect`],stem:`A company wants users from a partner organization to sign in to its apps with their own work accounts and to appear in its directory as guest users. Which capability should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`B2B direct connect`,why:`B2B direct connect uses a mutual trust with another Entra tenant, and its users aren’t added to your directory.`},{id:`b`,text:`Microsoft Entra Connect`,why:`Entra Connect syncs your own on-premises users to Entra ID, not partner users.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,why:`Domain Services provides a managed domain for legacy apps.`},{id:`d`,text:`B2B collaboration`,why:`Correct: B2B collaboration lets external users sign in with their own identities, and they usually appear as guest users.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`B2B collaboration adds guest users to your directory; B2B direct connect uses a two-way trust and doesn’t add them.`},{id:`q7.3-02`,objectiveId:`7.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about external identities, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`B2B direct connect users are added to your directory as guest users.`,answer:!1,why:`B2B direct connect users aren’t added to your directory; B2B collaboration users are.`},{id:`s2`,text:`An external user’s own identity provider handles authentication, and you control authorization to your apps.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn describes external identities this way.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Access reviews can confirm whether each guest user still needs access.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says guests or a decision maker can confirm whether each guest still needs access.`}],explanation:`External users bring their own identities; you decide what they can reach and can review guest access over time.`},{id:`q7.3-03`,objectiveId:`7.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company’s new app will be used by its own employees and administrators, and by staff from a supplier. Which of these is an external identity?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A new employee whose account is created in your tenant`,why:`Your own employees’ accounts are internal identities in your tenant.`},{id:`b`,text:`A supplier who signs in with their own work account`,why:`Correct: partners, suppliers, contractors, and customers outside your tenant are external identities.`},{id:`c`,text:`An administrator in your organization’s tenant`,why:`An administrator in your own tenant is an internal identity.`},{id:`d`,text:`A security group in your own directory`,why:`Groups in your directory hold your organization’s identities.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`An external identity is a person, device, or service outside your organization’s tenant, often using their own work, government, or social identity.`},{id:`q7.4-01`,objectiveId:`7.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`7.4|Conditional Access vs RBAC`],stem:`A company wants to require MFA when administrators sign in from outside its trusted networks, and to block sign-ins from unexpected countries. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure role-based access control`,why:`RBAC decides what an identity can do to Azure resources, not whether a sign-in is allowed.`},{id:`b`,text:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,why:`Defender for Cloud assesses security posture and raises threat alerts; it doesn’t evaluate sign-ins.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra Conditional Access`,why:`Correct: Conditional Access uses signals like location to allow access, require MFA, or block access.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Azure Policy enforces rules on resources, not on sign-ins.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Conditional Access decides whether a sign-in is allowed based on signals; RBAC decides what an identity can do to Azure resources.`},{id:`q7.4-02`,objectiveId:`7.4`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`During sign-in, Conditional Access collects signals, makes a decision based on them, and then ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`enforces the decision`,why:`Correct: the flow is signals → decision → enforcement (allow, require MFA, or block).`},{id:`b`,text:`assigns the user an Azure RBAC role`,why:`RBAC role assignments are made separately; Conditional Access enforces sign-in decisions.`},{id:`c`,text:`syncs the user to on-premises Active Directory`,why:`Syncing identities is Microsoft Entra Connect’s job.`},{id:`d`,text:`alerts Defender for Cloud`,why:`Defender for Cloud raises its own security alerts; Conditional Access enforces its sign-in decision.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Conditional Access enforces its decision by allowing access, requiring multifactor authentication, or blocking access.`},{id:`q7.4-03`,objectiveId:`7.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Microsoft Entra Conditional Access, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Conditional Access can block a sign-in entirely, not only require MFA.`,answer:!0,why:`Blocking access is one of the three outcomes.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Conditional Access decides which actions a user can perform on a resource group.`,answer:!1,why:`That is Azure RBAC; Conditional Access decides whether the sign-in is allowed.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Conditional Access looks only at the user’s password, not their location or device.`,answer:!1,why:`Its signals include the user, their location, their device, and the application.`}],explanation:`Conditional Access uses signals (user, location, device, app) to allow access, require MFA, or block, making MFA more granular.`},{id:`q7.5-01`,objectiveId:`7.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`7.5|Conditional Access`],stem:`A company assigns Azure roles to an engineers group. A new engineer joins and needs the same access to Azure resources as the rest of the team. What is the simplest approach?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Create a Conditional Access policy for the new engineer`,why:`Conditional Access decides whether sign-ins are allowed; it doesn’t grant permissions on Azure resources.`},{id:`b`,text:`Make the engineer an Owner`,why:`Owner grants far more than the team has, which breaks least privilege.`},{id:`c`,text:`Add the engineer to the engineers group`,why:`Correct: users in a group get all the permissions of the roles assigned to that group.`},{id:`d`,text:`Copy each teammate’s permissions to the engineer one by one`,why:`RBAC exists so you don’t manage permissions person by person.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Azure RBAC assigns roles to users or groups, so adding someone to a group gives them the group’s access.`},{id:`q7.5-02`,objectiveId:`7.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure RBAC, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`The Reader role assigned on a subscription lets a user view every resource in that subscription.`,answer:!0,why:`Permissions at a parent scope are inherited by every child scope.`},{id:`s2`,text:`When a user has two role assignments, only the more restrictive one applies.`,answer:!1,why:`RBAC uses an allow model: permissions from multiple assignments add together.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Azure RBAC controls access to the data inside your applications.`,answer:!1,why:`RBAC is enforced on actions through Azure Resource Manager, not at the application or data level.`}],explanation:`RBAC is hierarchical and additive, and it applies to Azure Resource Manager actions.`},{id:`q7.5-03`,objectiveId:`7.5`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`At which two of these scopes can you assign an Azure RBAC role? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A management group`,why:`Correct: management groups are one of the four RBAC scopes.`},{id:`b`,text:`An availability zone`,why:`Availability zones are physical locations, not RBAC scopes.`},{id:`c`,text:`An Azure region`,why:`Regions are physical locations, not RBAC scopes.`},{id:`d`,text:`A resource group`,why:`Correct: resource groups are one of the four RBAC scopes.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`RBAC scopes are management group, subscription, resource group, and single resource, and permissions are inherited downward.`},{id:`q7.6-01`,objectiveId:`7.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which is one of the three guiding principles of Zero Trust?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Trust devices on the corporate network`,why:`Zero Trust doesn’t trust anything based on network location.`},{id:`b`,text:`Grant broad access, then remove it when needed`,why:`Zero Trust uses least privilege access, the opposite.`},{id:`c`,text:`Verify once at the network perimeter`,why:`Zero Trust verifies every request, not once at the edge.`},{id:`d`,text:`Assume breach`,why:`Correct: the three principles are verify explicitly, use least privilege access, and assume breach.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Zero Trust assumes a breach has already happened and verifies each request as though it came from an uncontrolled network.`},{id:`q7.6-02`,objectiveId:`7.6`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`7.6|Perimeter-based security`],stem:`An employee signs in from an unmanaged laptop on public Wi-Fi. Under a Zero Trust model, what decides whether access is granted?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Whether the request comes from inside the corporate network`,why:`Trusting the inside of the network is perimeter-based security, which Zero Trust replaces.`},{id:`b`,text:`Authentication plus context such as device state and risk`,why:`Correct: access is based on authentication and context, not network location.`},{id:`c`,text:`Whether the employee already signed in earlier that day`,why:`Zero Trust verifies each request explicitly, not once a day.`},{id:`d`,text:`Whether the laptop’s IP address belongs to the company`,why:`Network location alone isn’t trusted under Zero Trust.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Perimeter security trusts anything inside the corporate network; Zero Trust checks identity, device state, and risk for every request.`},{id:`q7.6-03`,objectiveId:`7.6`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Limiting users with just-in-time and just-enough-access (JIT/JEA) is part of the Zero Trust principle of ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`least privilege access`,why:`Correct: least privilege access limits users with JIT/JEA, risk-based adaptive policies, and data protection.`},{id:`b`,text:`verifying explicitly`,why:`Verify explicitly means always authenticating and authorizing on all available data.`},{id:`c`,text:`segmenting the network perimeter`,why:`Zero Trust doesn’t rely on the perimeter; segmenting access is part of assume breach.`},{id:`d`,text:`assuming breach`,why:`Assume breach is about limiting impact, segmenting access, and detecting threats.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Use least privilege access: just-in-time and just-enough-access, risk-based adaptive policies, and data protection.`},{id:`q7.7-01`,objectiveId:`7.7`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each control to its defense-in-depth layer. Each layer may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`DDoS protection and perimeter firewalls`,why:`Learn puts DDoS protection in the perimeter layer.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Denying communication between resources by default`,why:`“Deny by default” belongs to the network layer.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Storing secrets in Azure Key Vault`,why:`Learn puts Key Vault secrets in the application layer.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Encryption at rest and in transit`,why:`Protecting the data itself with encryption is the data layer.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Perimeter`},{id:`t2`,text:`Network`},{id:`t3`,text:`Application`},{id:`t4`,text:`Data`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Outside in: physical security → identity and access → perimeter → network → compute → application → data.`},{id:`q7.7-02`,objectiveId:`7.7`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`framing`,covers:[`7.7|Defense in depth vs Zero Trust`],stem:`Which statement describes defense in depth rather than Zero Trust?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Verify every access request, wherever it comes from`,why:`Verifying every request regardless of location is Zero Trust.`},{id:`b`,text:`Grant access based on authentication, not network location`,why:`That is Zero Trust’s replacement for perimeter trust.`},{id:`c`,text:`Assume breach`,why:`Assume breach is one of Zero Trust’s three principles.`},{id:`d`,text:`Layer protections around the data`,why:`Correct: defense in depth stacks protective layers, with data at the center.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Defense in depth stacks protective layers around data; Zero Trust verifies every access request instead of trusting network location.`},{id:`q7.7-03`,objectiveId:`7.7`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`For each statement about the defense-in-depth model, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Microsoft Learn’s model has seven layers, with data at the center.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists seven layers, from physical security outside to data at the center.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Physical security is the innermost layer.`,answer:!1,why:`Physical security is the outermost layer; data is the innermost.`},{id:`s3`,text:`If one layer is breached, the next layer is still protecting the data.`,answer:!0,why:`That is the purpose of the model: no single layer is relied on.`}],explanation:`Defense in depth puts a series of layers around the data, so a breach of one layer is slowed by the next.`},{id:`q7.8-01`,objectiveId:`7.8`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`7.8|Azure Policy`],stem:`A security team wants a secure score for its Azure resources, recommendations to harden them, and alerts when threats are detected. Which service provides this?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Azure Policy enforces rules on resources; Defender for Cloud builds on it to assess posture and raise threat alerts.`},{id:`b`,text:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,why:`Correct: Defender for Cloud is security posture management and threat protection, with a secure score and alerts.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra Conditional Access`,why:`Conditional Access controls sign-ins; it doesn’t score resource security.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra ID`,why:`Entra ID is identity and access management, not resource security posture.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Defender for Cloud continuously assesses, secures, and defends: secure score and recommendations for posture, plus alerts for threats.`},{id:`q7.8-02`,objectiveId:`7.8`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Microsoft Defender for Cloud, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Defender for Cloud can protect resources in other clouds, such as AWS and Google Cloud.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says it can protect resources in AWS and GCP.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Defender for Cloud can’t monitor on-premises machines.`,answer:!1,why:`It can, when you deploy Azure Arc and enable its enhanced security features.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Because it’s native to Azure, Defender for Cloud can monitor many Azure services without extra deployment.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says many Azure services are monitored without extra deployment.`}],explanation:`Defender for Cloud covers Azure, on-premises (through Azure Arc), hybrid, and multicloud resources.`},{id:`q7.8-03`,objectiveId:`7.8`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Defender for Cloud meets three needs: continuously assess, secure, and ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`govern`,why:`Governance is Azure Policy’s and Microsoft Purview’s area; Defender’s third need is defend.`},{id:`b`,text:`migrate`,why:`Migration is Azure Migrate’s job.`},{id:`c`,text:`defend`,why:`Correct: assess, secure, and defend. Defend means detecting threats and raising security alerts.`},{id:`d`,text:`deploy`,why:`Deploying resources isn’t one of Defender for Cloud’s three needs.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Assess: know your posture. Secure: harden resources against the Microsoft cloud security benchmark. Defend: detect threats and raise alerts.`}],u=[{id:`q8.1-01`,objectiveId:`8.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A team deleted several VMs it no longer needed, but its Azure bill barely went down. What is the most likely reason?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Deleted VMs are billed until the end of the year`,why:`Azure charges for what you use each billing cycle; there’s no year-end charge for deleted VMs.`},{id:`b`,text:`The VMs’ disks and networking resources were left behind`,why:`Correct: deprovisioning a VM may leave its storage and networking resources behind, and they keep costing money.`},{id:`c`,text:`VMs in a resource group can’t be deleted`,why:`VMs in a resource group can be deleted; the group itself isn’t what keeps billing.`},{id:`d`,text:`Their region charges a deletion fee`,why:`Regions have different prices, but there’s no deletion fee.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Maintenance affects cost: when you remove a VM, check for leftover disks, IP addresses, and other resources that still bill.`},{id:`q8.1-02`,objectiveId:`8.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure costs, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`The same resource can cost different amounts in different Azure regions.`,answer:!0,why:`Prices differ by region because of power, labor, taxes, and fees.`},{id:`s2`,text:`All data transferred into Azure datacenters is free.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says some inbound data transfer is free, not all of it.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Outbound data transfer, leaving Azure datacenters, is priced by billing zone.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says outbound transfer is priced based on billing zones.`}],explanation:`Geography and network traffic both affect cost: regional prices differ, some inbound transfer is free, and outbound transfer is priced by billing zone.`},{id:`q8.1-03`,objectiveId:`8.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`8.1|Billing zones vs availability zones`],stem:`In Azure pricing, what is a billing zone?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A physically separate datacenter group within one region`,why:`That describes an availability zone, which is about resiliency, not pricing.`},{id:`b`,text:`The paired region used for disaster recovery`,why:`Region pairs are about disaster recovery, not data transfer pricing.`},{id:`c`,text:`A group of regions used to price data transfer`,why:`Correct: billing zones group regions for data-transfer pricing.`},{id:`d`,text:`A spending limit set on a subscription`,why:`A spending limit is set with a budget or the subscription type, not a billing zone.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`A billing zone is a group of regions used for data-transfer pricing; an availability zone is a physically separate part of one region.`},{id:`q8.1-04`,objectiveId:`8.1`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two settings of a virtual machine affect its cost? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The number of processor cores`,why:`Correct: Learn lists processor cores among the VM settings that affect cost.`},{id:`b`,text:`The name of its resource group`,why:`A resource group’s name has no effect on price.`},{id:`c`,text:`The licensing it uses`,why:`Correct: Learn lists licensing among the VM settings that affect cost.`},{id:`d`,text:`The number of tags applied to it`,why:`Tags are metadata; they help you report costs but don’t change them.`}],answer:[`a`,`c`],explanation:`Resource type and settings matter: a VM’s cost depends on licensing, processor cores, attached storage, and its network interface.`},{id:`q8.1-05`,objectiveId:`8.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A student signs up for an Azure free trial that includes credits. This shows how the ___ can affect what you pay.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`region you choose`,why:`Region affects prices, but a free trial with credits is a subscription type.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Marketplace`,why:`Marketplace purchases are a separate cost factor.`},{id:`c`,text:`resource group you use`,why:`Resource groups don’t change what you pay.`},{id:`d`,text:`subscription type`,why:`Correct: Learn lists subscription type, such as a free trial with credits, as a cost factor.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Cost factors include resource type, consumption, maintenance, geography, subscription type, and Azure Marketplace.`},{id:`q8.2-01`,objectiveId:`8.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,verify:`Microsoft Learn says the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) calculator has been retired, though older study guides still mention it. It appears here only as a wrong option.`,covers:[`8.2|TCO calculator`],stem:`Before deploying anything, a team wants to estimate the monthly cost of a new Azure solution with VMs, storage, and network bandwidth. Which tool should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The TCO calculator`,why:`Microsoft Learn says the TCO calculator has been retired; the current outline names only the pricing calculator.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements to resources you’ve already deployed.`},{id:`c`,text:`The pricing calculator`,why:`Correct: the pricing calculator estimates the cost of Azure resources before you deploy them.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Cost Management budgets`,why:`Budgets track actual spending after you deploy.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`The pricing calculator is the answer for estimating costs before you deploy, including compute, storage, and network costs.`},{id:`q8.2-02`,objectiveId:`8.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`8.2|Microsoft Cost Management`],stem:`A finance team wants to see what the company actually spent last month in each resource group. Which should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The pricing calculator`,why:`The pricing calculator estimates costs before you deploy; it doesn’t show actual spending.`},{id:`b`,text:`Cost analysis in Microsoft Cost Management`,why:`Correct: cost analysis shows actual costs by resource group, region, service, and more.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Service Health`,why:`Service Health reports on Azure outages and maintenance, not spending.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Policy compliance`,why:`Azure Policy reports on configuration rules, not costs.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`The pricing calculator estimates costs before you deploy; Cost Management analyzes and controls actual spending after you deploy.`},{id:`q8.2-03`,objectiveId:`8.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about the Azure pricing calculator, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Adding a VM to an estimate in the pricing calculator creates that VM in your subscription.`,answer:!1,why:`Nothing is provisioned when you add resources to the calculator.`},{id:`s2`,text:`The prices in the pricing calculator are estimates.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says the calculator is for information purposes only, and prices are estimates.`},{id:`s3`,text:`An estimate can include network costs as well as compute and storage.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says estimates can include compute, storage, and associated network costs.`}],explanation:`The pricing calculator is information only: nothing is provisioned or charged, and estimates can cover compute, storage, and network costs.`},{id:`q8.2-04`,objectiveId:`8.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Teams often use a pricing calculator estimate as the baseline for ___ before they deploy.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`budget approval`,why:`Correct: an estimate is a common baseline for getting a budget approved, revisited when the design changes.`},{id:`b`,text:`resource locks on the deployment`,why:`Locks protect resources from deletion or changes; they have nothing to do with estimates.`},{id:`c`,text:`RBAC roles`,why:`Role assignments decide who can act on resources, not what they cost.`},{id:`d`,text:`the Azure Advisor score`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements to deployed resources; it doesn’t use calculator estimates.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`An estimate from the pricing calculator helps get a budget approved before deployment.`},{id:`q8.2-05`,objectiveId:`8.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two can you do in the Azure pricing calculator? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`See what you actually spent last month`,why:`Actual spending is in Microsoft Cost Management, not the calculator.`},{id:`b`,text:`Start an estimate from an example scenario`,why:`Correct: you can price individual resources, a whole solution, or start from an example scenario.`},{id:`c`,text:`Compare storage redundancy options`,why:`Correct: you can compare options such as storage type, access tier, and redundancy.`},{id:`d`,text:`Send an alert when spending reaches a threshold`,why:`Spending alerts come from budgets in Cost Management.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`The calculator estimates costs: price resources or a solution, start from a scenario, and compare options such as redundancy and region.`},{id:`q8.3-01`,objectiveId:`8.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`8.3|Budget vs spending cap`],stem:`A company wants to be notified when spending on a resource group reaches a monthly amount it sets. What should it create?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A resource lock on the resource group`,why:`Locks prevent deletion or changes; they don’t track spending.`},{id:`b`,text:`A pricing calculator estimate`,why:`An estimate is made before you deploy; it doesn’t watch actual spending.`},{id:`c`,text:`An Azure Advisor recommendation`,why:`Advisor suggests ways to reduce costs, but it doesn’t alert on a spending amount you set.`},{id:`d`,text:`A budget with an alert threshold`,why:`Correct: a budget sets a spending limit, and a budget alert fires when spending reaches the threshold.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`A budget alerts you at a threshold and can trigger automation, but on its own it doesn’t stop resources or block spending.`},{id:`q8.3-02`,objectiveId:`8.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Microsoft Cost Management, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A budget on its own automatically stops resources when the budget is exceeded.`,answer:!1,why:`A budget alerts you; stopping resources needs automation that the budget triggers.`},{id:`s2`,text:`A budget can trigger automation, such as shutting down nonproduction resources.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says budgets can trigger automation like this.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Cost Management helps catch surprises, such as accidentally provisioned resources, before the invoice arrives.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says this is a purpose of Cost Management.`}],explanation:`Budgets alert and can trigger automation, so cost surprises show up before the invoice rather than on it.`},{id:`q8.3-03`,objectiveId:`8.3`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each situation to the type of cost alert it produces. Each alert type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Spending reaches a threshold you set in a budget`,why:`That is a budget alert.`},{id:`i2`,text:`An Enterprise Agreement’s Azure Prepayment is being used up`,why:`That is a credit alert.`},{id:`i3`,text:`An EA department’s spending reaches a set share of its quota`,why:`That is a department spending quota alert.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Budget alert`},{id:`t2`,text:`Credit alert`},{id:`t3`,text:`Department spending quota alert`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`},explanation:`Cost alerts gather three types in one place: budget alerts, credit alerts, and department spending quota alerts (the last two for Enterprise Agreement customers).`},{id:`q8.3-04`,objectiveId:`8.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In Microsoft Cost Management, ___ gives a visual of accumulated costs over time, compared against a budget.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`the pricing calculator`,why:`The pricing calculator estimates costs before you deploy.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Service Health`,why:`Service Health reports on the health of Azure services.`},{id:`c`,text:`cost analysis`,why:`Correct: cost analysis visualizes costs and shows accumulated costs against a budget.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Monitor Log Analytics`,why:`Log Analytics queries log data, not billing.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Cost analysis gives a quick visual of costs by billing cycle, region, resource, and more, including accumulated costs against a budget.`},{id:`q8.3-05`,objectiveId:`8.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`What can an Azure budget be set for?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A subscription, resource group, or service type`,why:`Correct: Learn says budgets can be set by subscription, resource group, service type, or other criteria.`},{id:`b`,text:`Only an entire Azure account, never anything smaller`,why:`Budgets can be narrower, such as one resource group.`},{id:`c`,text:`Only a single virtual machine`,why:`Budgets aren’t limited to one resource type.`},{id:`d`,text:`Only a management group`,why:`Budgets can be set at other levels too, such as a subscription or resource group.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`You can scope a budget to a subscription, resource group, service type, or other criteria, then get alerts as spending approaches it.`},{id:`q8.4-01`,objectiveId:`8.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`8.4|Tags vs resource groups`],stem:`A company wants to report costs by department. Each department’s resources are spread across several resource groups and subscriptions, and the company doesn’t want to move them. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A single resource group per department`,why:`That would mean moving resources, and a resource can be in only one resource group.`},{id:`b`,text:`Tags such as Department = Finance`,why:`Correct: a resource can carry many tags, so tags group resources across resource groups and subscriptions.`},{id:`c`,text:`Resource locks on each department’s resources`,why:`Locks prevent deletion or changes; they don’t group costs.`},{id:`d`,text:`Separate regions`,why:`Regions are physical locations; moving resources between regions isn’t a reporting tool.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Tags are name-value metadata that let you group resources, and their costs, across resource groups and subscriptions.`},{id:`q8.4-02`,objectiveId:`8.4`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure tags, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A tag is a name-value pair, such as Environment = Prod.`,answer:!0,why:`Tags are name-value pairs of metadata.`},{id:`s2`,text:`By default, resources inherit the tags applied to their resource group.`,answer:!1,why:`Resources don’t inherit tags from their resource group or subscription by default.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Tags can be applied only to individual resources.`,answer:!1,why:`Tags can be applied at the resource, resource group, or subscription level.`}],explanation:`Tags are name-value pairs at the resource, resource group, or subscription level, and they don’t flow down to resources by default.`},{id:`q8.4-03`,objectiveId:`8.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants every new resource to have a CostCenter tag, and wants the tag put back automatically if someone removes it. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure RBAC`,why:`RBAC controls what users can do; it doesn’t require or restore tags.`},{id:`b`,text:`A resource lock`,why:`A lock blocks deletes or changes, but it doesn’t add or restore tags.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t enforce tagging.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Correct: Azure Policy can require a tag on new resources and reapply a tag that was removed.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Tag enforcement is a governance job: Azure Policy can require tags on new resources or reapply tags that were removed.`},{id:`q8.4-04`,objectiveId:`8.4`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two are uses of tags that Microsoft Learn lists? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Encrypting data at rest`,why:`Tags are metadata; they don’t encrypt anything.`},{id:`b`,text:`Security classification`,why:`Correct: Learn lists security classification among the uses of tags.`},{id:`c`,text:`Workload optimization and automation`,why:`Correct: Learn lists workload optimization and automation among the uses of tags.`},{id:`d`,text:`Signing users in with MFA`,why:`Sign-in is handled by Microsoft Entra ID, not tags.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`Tag uses: resource management, cost management, operations, security classification, compliance, and workload optimization and automation.`},{id:`q8.4-05`,objectiveId:`8.4`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`You can add, change, or delete tags in the Azure portal, Azure PowerShell, the Azure CLI, ARM templates, or ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t add or change tags.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure REST API`,why:`Correct: Learn lists the REST API among the ways to manage tags.`},{id:`c`,text:`the Service Health dashboard`,why:`Service Health reports on Azure’s health; it doesn’t manage tags.`},{id:`d`,text:`the Microsoft Purview portal`,why:`Purview governs data; it isn’t where you manage Azure resource tags.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Tags can be managed with the portal, PowerShell, the Azure CLI, ARM templates, or the REST API.`}],d=[{id:`q9.1-01`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which statement best describes Microsoft Purview?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A service that enforces rules on resource settings`,why:`That describes Azure Policy.`},{id:`b`,text:`A security posture and threat protection service for cloud workloads`,why:`That describes Microsoft Defender for Cloud.`},{id:`c`,text:`A family of data governance, risk, and compliance solutions`,why:`Correct: that is how Learn describes Microsoft Purview.`},{id:`d`,text:`A tool that extends Azure management to on-premises and other clouds`,why:`That describes Azure Arc.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Microsoft Purview is a family of data governance, risk, and compliance solutions with a unified view of your data.`},{id:`q9.1-02`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company stores customer data in Azure, in on-premises SQL Server databases, and in Amazon S3. It needs to find where sensitive data is stored across all of them. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Microsoft Purview`,why:`Correct: Purview maps data across Azure, on-premises, and other clouds such as Amazon S3, and classifies sensitive data.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Azure Policy evaluates how Azure resources are configured, not what data they hold.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Azure Arc manages servers and other resources outside Azure; it doesn’t classify the data in them.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Monitor Log Analytics`,why:`Log Analytics queries logs and metrics collected from your resources; it doesn’t discover or classify the sensitive data they store.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Purview’s unified data governance helps you map your whole data estate and find where sensitive data is stored.`},{id:`q9.1-03`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Microsoft Purview’s risk and compliance solutions build on ___ services such as Teams, OneDrive, and Exchange.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Azure Arc manages non-Azure resources; Teams, OneDrive, and Exchange are Microsoft 365.`},{id:`b`,text:`Microsoft 365`,why:`Correct: Purview’s risk and compliance solutions build on Microsoft 365 services.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Monitor`,why:`Azure Monitor collects logs and metrics; it isn’t the suite that includes Teams and Exchange.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure DevOps`,why:`Azure DevOps is a developer service, not the suite that includes Teams and Exchange.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Risk and compliance solutions use Microsoft 365 services to protect sensitive data, identify data risks, and manage regulatory compliance.`},{id:`q9.1-04`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to map its entire data estate, including SQL and Hive databases, on-premises data, and data in other clouds. Which part of Microsoft Purview fits this goal?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Risk and compliance solutions`,why:`Risk and compliance builds on Microsoft 365 to protect sensitive data and manage regulations; mapping the data estate is unified data governance.`},{id:`b`,text:`Policy initiatives`,why:`Azure Policy initiatives group policy definitions for resource configuration; they aren’t part of Purview.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra ID Governance`,why:`Entra governs identities and access, not a map of data sources.`},{id:`d`,text:`Unified data governance`,why:`Correct: unified data governance maps your entire data estate across Azure, databases, on-premises, and other clouds.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Purview has two main solution areas: risk and compliance (built on Microsoft 365), and unified data governance (mapping and managing data everywhere).`},{id:`q9.1-05`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Microsoft Purview, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Purview can give a unified view of data in SaaS apps, not only on-premises and in the cloud.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says Purview covers data on-premises, in multiple clouds, and in software-as-a-service apps.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Purview can only find data that someone has catalogued by hand.`,answer:!1,why:`Purview offers automated data discovery.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Purview can help manage access to data at scale.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn lists managing access to data at scale among unified data governance’s goals.`}],explanation:`Purview automatically discovers data across on-premises, multicloud, and SaaS, and helps manage access to it at scale.`},{id:`q9.1-06`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two capabilities does Microsoft Purview provide? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`End-to-end data lineage`,why:`Correct: Learn lists end-to-end data lineage among Purview’s capabilities.`},{id:`b`,text:`Restricting which VM sizes can be deployed`,why:`Restricting resource configuration is Azure Policy.`},{id:`c`,text:`Blocking deletion of a resource group`,why:`Blocking deletion is what resource locks do.`},{id:`d`,text:`Helping data consumers find valuable data securely`,why:`Correct: unified data governance helps data consumers find valuable data securely.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`Purview keeps you current on your data landscape with discovery, classification, and lineage, and helps people find the data they need.`},{id:`q9.1-07`,objectiveId:`9.1`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`9.1|Azure Policy`],stem:`Match each need to the service that meets it. Each service may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`Govern the data itself, wherever it’s stored`,why:`Governing data, rather than resource settings, is Microsoft Purview.`},{id:`i2`,text:`Require that every new storage account uses a specific redundancy setting`,why:`Controlling how resources are configured is Azure Policy.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Let the finance team view resources but not change them`,why:`Deciding what a group of users can do is Azure RBAC.`},{id:`i4`,text:`Allow a contractor to restart VMs in one resource group`,why:`Granting a person permission to do something is Azure RBAC.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Microsoft Purview`},{id:`t2`,text:`Azure Policy`},{id:`t3`,text:`Azure RBAC`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t3`},explanation:`Azure Policy governs how Azure resources are configured; Microsoft Purview governs the data itself, wherever it is stored; RBAC governs what users can do.`},{id:`q9.2-01`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to allow only certain VM sizes to be deployed in its subscription. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A resource lock`,why:`Locks prevent deletion or changes; they don’t restrict which sizes can be deployed.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Correct: Azure Policy can prevent noncompliant resources, such as disallowed VM sizes, from being created.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t enforce your rules.`},{id:`d`,text:`Tags`,why:`Tags label resources; they don’t restrict what can be deployed.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure Policy controls or audits resource configurations, and it can block noncompliant resources from being created.`},{id:`q9.2-02`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In Azure Policy, a group of related policy definitions that tracks compliance with a larger goal is called ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`an initiative`,why:`Correct: an initiative groups related policy definitions for a larger compliance goal.`},{id:`b`,text:`an action group`,why:`Action groups decide who is notified by Azure Monitor alerts.`},{id:`c`,text:`a tag`,why:`A tag is name-value metadata on a resource, not a group of policies.`},{id:`d`,text:`an ARM template`,why:`An ARM template describes resources to deploy, not a set of policies.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Initiatives bundle related policy definitions, so you can track compliance with a larger goal in one place.`},{id:`q9.2-03`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure Policy, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`You can assign a policy to a single resource, not only to a subscription.`,answer:!0,why:`Policies can be set at any level, such as a subscription, a resource group, or a single resource.`},{id:`s2`,text:`A policy assigned to a resource group doesn’t apply to the resources inside it.`,answer:!1,why:`Policies are inherited by everything below the scope where they’re set.`},{id:`s3`,text:`A policy that allows only certain VM sizes also checks VMs when they’re resized.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says such a policy checks new VMs, resized VMs, and all current VMs.`}],explanation:`Policies can be set at any level, are inherited downward, and are checked whenever resources are created or changed.`},{id:`q9.2-04`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A policy requires a CostCenter tag. Some resources created before the policy don’t have the tag. What can Azure Policy do about them?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Delete and recreate them`,why:`Policy doesn’t delete existing resources.`},{id:`b`,text:`Nothing, because policies only check new resources`,why:`Policy evaluates existing resources too.`},{id:`c`,text:`Flag them, and in some cases add the tag`,why:`Correct: existing resources are flagged as noncompliant, and in some cases Policy can fix them, such as adding a missing tag.`},{id:`d`,text:`Lock them until someone adds the tag by hand`,why:`Policy doesn’t apply resource locks.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Noncompliant existing resources are flagged; some can be remediated automatically, and resources can be marked as exceptions.`},{id:`q9.2-05`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`9.2|Azure RBAC`],stem:`Engineers have the Contributor role and must keep creating VMs. The company wants to make sure that, whoever creates them, VMs are created only in its two approved regions. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Remove the engineers’ Contributor role`,why:`That would stop them creating VMs anywhere; RBAC controls what users can do, not where resources may go.`},{id:`b`,text:`A resource lock on the subscription`,why:`A lock blocks deletes or changes; it doesn’t restrict locations.`},{id:`c`,text:`Tags`,why:`Tags are labels; they don’t enforce where resources go.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Correct: Azure Policy controls how resources may be configured, no matter who creates them.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`RBAC controls what users can do; Azure Policy controls how resources may be configured, such as allowed locations.`},{id:`q9.2-06`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two statements about Azure Policy are true? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`It decides whether a user’s sign-in needs MFA`,why:`That is Microsoft Entra Conditional Access.`},{id:`b`,text:`It evaluates resources that existed before the policy was created`,why:`Correct: Policy evaluates existing resources too, not only new ones.`},{id:`c`,text:`It encrypts the data stored in resources`,why:`Policy governs configuration; encryption is done by the services themselves.`},{id:`d`,text:`It lets you mark specific resources as exceptions`,why:`Correct: Learn says resources can be marked as exceptions.`}],answer:[`b`,`d`],explanation:`Azure Policy evaluates new and existing resources, and specific resources can be marked as exceptions.`},{id:`q9.2-07`,objectiveId:`9.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants a report of which of its existing resources don’t meet the configuration standards it defined, without Azure changing anything yet. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Policy compliance evaluation`,why:`Correct: Azure Policy evaluates resources against your policies and highlights the ones that aren’t compliant.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends Microsoft’s best practices; it doesn’t check resources against standards you define.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Service Health`,why:`Service Health reports on Azure’s health, not your configurations.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Purview`,why:`Purview governs data, not how Azure resources are configured.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Azure Policy can audit as well as enforce: it evaluates resources and highlights the noncompliant ones.`},{id:`q9.3-01`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company must stop a production database from being deleted by accident, but administrators still need to change its settings. What should it apply?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A ReadOnly lock`,why:`A ReadOnly lock would also block setting changes.`},{id:`b`,text:`The Reader role for all administrators`,why:`Reader would stop administrators changing settings, too.`},{id:`c`,text:`A Delete lock`,why:`Correct: a Delete lock lets authorized users read and modify a resource, but not delete it.`},{id:`d`,text:`A tag`,why:`Tags label resources; they don’t prevent deletion.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`A Delete lock blocks deletion but allows changes; a ReadOnly lock blocks both.`},{id:`q9.3-02`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`A ___ lock lets authorized users read a resource, but not update or delete it.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Delete`,why:`A Delete lock still allows updates; it only blocks deletion.`},{id:`b`,text:`Reader`,why:`Reader is an RBAC role, not a lock type.`},{id:`c`,text:`Contributor`,why:`Contributor is an RBAC role, not a lock type.`},{id:`d`,text:`ReadOnly`,why:`Correct: a ReadOnly lock allows reading but blocks updates and deletion.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`The two lock types are Delete (modify but not delete) and ReadOnly (read only).`},{id:`q9.3-03`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about resource locks, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`A lock on a resource group applies to every resource in that group.`,answer:!0,why:`Locks are inherited.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Locks can be applied only to individual resources.`,answer:!1,why:`Locks can be applied to a resource, a resource group, or a whole subscription.`},{id:`s3`,text:`If RBAC is set up correctly, locks add no extra protection.`,answer:!1,why:`Even with RBAC, people with enough access could still delete critical resources; locks add protection on top.`}],explanation:`Locks work at the resource, resource group, or subscription level, are inherited, and add protection on top of RBAC.`},{id:`q9.3-04`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`9.3|Resource locks vs RBAC`],stem:`A company must stop everyone, including users with the Owner role, from changing or deleting a set of resources until the protection is deliberately removed. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A ReadOnly lock`,why:`Correct: locks apply regardless of RBAC permissions, so even an Owner must remove the lock first.`},{id:`b`,text:`The Reader role for each user`,why:`The Reader role limits the identities it’s assigned to; Owners would keep their own permissions.`},{id:`c`,text:`A Delete lock`,why:`A Delete lock still allows changes.`},{id:`d`,text:`Conditional Access`,why:`Conditional Access decides whether sign-ins are allowed; it doesn’t protect specific resources.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`RBAC decides who may act on a resource; a lock blocks delete or change actions for everyone, even users whose role allows them.`},{id:`q9.3-05`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`An administrator needs to delete a resource group that has a Delete lock. What must happen first?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`The administrator is given the Owner role`,why:`Locks apply regardless of RBAC, so even an Owner is blocked.`},{id:`b`,text:`The lock is removed`,why:`Correct: changing or deleting a locked resource is two steps: remove the lock, then act.`},{id:`c`,text:`The lock expires after its set period`,why:`Locks don’t expire; someone must remove them.`},{id:`d`,text:`Its resources are moved to another group`,why:`Moving resources doesn’t remove the lock from the group you want to delete.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`To delete or change a locked resource, you first remove the lock, then perform the action.`},{id:`q9.3-06`,objectiveId:`9.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`A ReadOnly lock is applied to a resource group. Which two actions on a VM in that group are blocked? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Deleting the VM`,why:`Correct: a ReadOnly lock blocks deletion, and the VM inherits the group’s lock.`},{id:`b`,text:`Viewing the VM’s settings`,why:`A ReadOnly lock still allows reading.`},{id:`c`,text:`Changing the VM’s size`,why:`Correct: a ReadOnly lock blocks updates, and the VM inherits the group’s lock.`},{id:`d`,text:`Listing the resources in the group`,why:`Listing is reading, which is still allowed.`}],answer:[`a`,`c`],explanation:`A ReadOnly lock on a group is inherited by its resources: they can be read, but not updated or deleted.`}],f=[{id:`q10.1-01`,objectiveId:`10.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`10.1|Portal vs command-line tools`],stem:`A new administrator wants to create and manage Azure resources through a graphical, point-and-click interface in a web browser. Which should they use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure PowerShell cmdlets`,why:`Azure PowerShell is command-line: you run cmdlets, not point and click.`},{id:`b`,text:`The Azure CLI`,why:`The Azure CLI is command-line: you run Bash commands.`},{id:`c`,text:`An ARM template`,why:`An ARM template is a JSON file describing resources, not a graphical interface.`},{id:`d`,text:`The Azure portal`,why:`Correct: the portal is a web-based, unified console with a graphical user interface.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`The portal is a point-and-click interface; the Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell let you capture tasks in repeatable, automatable scripts.`},{id:`q10.1-02`,objectiveId:`10.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about the Azure portal, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`The Azure portal has a presence in every Azure datacenter.`,answer:!0,why:`That makes it resilient to individual datacenter failures and keeps it close to users.`},{id:`s2`,text:`The Azure portal needs scheduled downtime for maintenance updates.`,answer:!1,why:`The portal updates continuously with no downtime for maintenance.`},{id:`s3`,text:`You can create custom dashboards in the Azure portal.`,answer:!0,why:`Custom dashboards give an organized view of your resources.`}],explanation:`The portal is designed for resiliency and continuous availability, and you can build custom dashboards in it.`},{id:`q10.1-03`,objectiveId:`10.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`You open Azure Cloud Shell by selecting the Cloud Shell icon in ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor shows recommendations; Cloud Shell is opened from the portal.`},{id:`b`,text:`the Azure portal`,why:`Correct: you open Cloud Shell from the Azure portal by selecting its icon.`},{id:`c`,text:`an ARM template`,why:`Templates are files that describe resources; they don’t open a shell.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Monitor Log Analytics`,why:`Log Analytics is for querying log data.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`The portal is where you open Cloud Shell, then choose Azure PowerShell or the Azure CLI.`},{id:`q10.1-04`,objectiveId:`10.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`An administrator performs the same configuration task every week across many subscriptions and wants it to be repeatable and automatable. Which approach fits best?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Clicking through the task in the Azure portal each week`,why:`The portal is point-and-click; repeating the task by hand isn’t automation.`},{id:`b`,text:`Creating a custom portal dashboard for the task`,why:`Dashboards organize views of resources; they don’t perform tasks.`},{id:`c`,text:`A script in Azure PowerShell or the Azure CLI`,why:`Correct: both let you capture the task in a repeatable, automatable script.`},{id:`d`,text:`An Azure Advisor recommendation`,why:`Advisor suggests improvements; it doesn’t run your tasks.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Choose the portal for point-and-click work; choose the Azure CLI or Azure PowerShell for scripting and automation.`},{id:`q10.2-01`,objectiveId:`10.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`10.2|Cloud Shell vs Azure CLI`],stem:`A consultant must run Azure commands from a borrowed laptop. They can’t install software, and they want a shell that’s already signed in with their Azure credentials. What should they use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Cloud Shell`,why:`Correct: Cloud Shell is browser-based, needs no local installation, and is already authenticated.`},{id:`b`,text:`The Azure CLI installed on the laptop`,why:`That needs a local installation, which the consultant can’t do.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure PowerShell installed on the laptop`,why:`That also needs a local installation.`},{id:`d`,text:`An ARM template`,why:`A template describes resources to deploy; it isn’t a shell to run commands in.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Cloud Shell is where you run commands, in the browser; the Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell are the command sets you run in it.`},{id:`q10.2-02`,objectiveId:`10.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure command-line tools, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure PowerShell and the Azure CLI are functionally equivalent; the main difference is syntax.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says they’re functionally equivalent, so the choice comes down to which language you know.`},{id:`s2`,text:`You must install software on your computer before you can use Azure Cloud Shell.`,answer:!1,why:`Cloud Shell runs in the browser with no local installation.`},{id:`s3`,text:`The Azure CLI can be installed only on Linux.`,answer:!1,why:`Both tools can be installed on Windows, Linux, and macOS.`}],explanation:`Cloud Shell needs no install; both command-line tools run in it and install on Windows, Linux, and macOS.`},{id:`q10.2-03`,objectiveId:`10.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Azure PowerShell runs commands called ___, which call the Azure REST API.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`templates`,why:`Templates are files that describe resources; PowerShell commands are cmdlets.`},{id:`b`,text:`action groups`,why:`Action groups are used by Azure Monitor alerts.`},{id:`c`,text:`initiatives`,why:`Initiatives are groups of Azure Policy definitions.`},{id:`d`,text:`cmdlets`,why:`Correct: Azure PowerShell commands are cmdlets.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Azure PowerShell runs cmdlets; the Azure CLI runs Bash commands. Both call the Azure REST API.`},{id:`q10.2-04`,objectiveId:`10.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A team already writes Bash scripts and prefers Bash syntax. In Cloud Shell, which command set should it choose?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure PowerShell`,why:`PowerShell does the same jobs with cmdlets; the team prefers Bash syntax.`},{id:`b`,text:`The Azure CLI`,why:`Correct: the Azure CLI uses Bash commands. It can do the same jobs as PowerShell; the choice is about syntax.`},{id:`c`,text:`Bicep`,why:`Bicep is a declarative template language, not a command set you choose in Cloud Shell.`},{id:`d`,text:`An ARM template`,why:`An ARM template is a JSON file, not a command set.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Azure PowerShell and the Azure CLI are functionally equivalent, so the choice mostly comes down to which language you know.`},{id:`q10.3-01`,objectiveId:`10.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`10.3|Azure Arc vs Azure Migrate`],stem:`A company’s servers will stay in its on-premises datacenter and in another public cloud. It wants to manage them and apply Azure Policy to them as if they were running in Azure. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Migrate`,why:`Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure; these servers are staying where they are.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Data Box ships data physically; it doesn’t manage servers.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Correct: Azure Arc extends Azure management and governance to resources outside Azure, where they already run.`},{id:`d`,text:`A VPN`,why:`A VPN gateway connects networks; it doesn’t let you manage servers with Azure tools.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Azure Arc manages resources where they already run; Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure.`},{id:`q10.3-02`,objectiveId:`10.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,verify:`Learn lists virtual machines as a preview capability of Azure Arc. This question doesn’t depend on it: virtual machines aren’t among the options.`,stem:`Which two kinds of resources outside Azure can Azure Arc manage? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Microsoft 365 mailboxes`,why:`Mailboxes are Microsoft 365 content, not a resource type Arc manages.`},{id:`b`,text:`Kubernetes clusters`,why:`Correct: Learn lists Kubernetes clusters among the resources Arc manages.`},{id:`c`,text:`Servers`,why:`Correct: Learn lists servers among the resources Arc manages.`},{id:`d`,text:`Microsoft Entra user accounts`,why:`User accounts are identities in Entra ID, not resources Arc projects into Azure.`}],answer:[`b`,`c`],explanation:`Learn lists servers, Kubernetes clusters, Azure data services, SQL Server, and VMs (in preview) as what Arc can manage outside Azure.`},{id:`q10.3-03`,objectiveId:`10.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Azure Arc works by projecting your existing non-Azure resources into ___.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Resource Manager`,why:`Correct: Arc projects non-Azure resources into ARM so you can manage them with Azure tools.`},{id:`b`,text:`an Azure Migrate project`,why:`Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure; Arc leaves them where they are.`},{id:`c`,text:`Log Analytics`,why:`Log Analytics queries collected logs; it isn’t how Arc manages resources.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Data Box`,why:`Data Box ships data physically.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`By projecting resources into Azure Resource Manager, Arc lets you manage them as if they were running in Azure.`},{id:`q10.3-04`,objectiveId:`10.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure Arc, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure Arc gives one consistent way to apply governance and track inventory across Azure, on-premises, and multicloud.`,answer:!0,why:`That’s the purpose Learn describes, instead of disconnected tools for each environment.`},{id:`s2`,text:`With Azure Arc, you manage non-Azure resources with separate tools from the ones you use for Azure.`,answer:!1,why:`Arc lets you manage them with the same Azure services and tools.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud uses Azure Arc to protect on-premises machines.`,answer:!0,why:`Deploying Arc is how Defender for Cloud covers on-premises machines.`}],explanation:`Arc brings non-Azure resources under the same Azure management, governance, and security tools.`},{id:`q10.4-01`,objectiveId:`10.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`10.4|Imperative vs declarative`],stem:`Which statement describes a declarative approach to infrastructure as code?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`You list each command to run, in order`,why:`Step-by-step commands are the imperative approach.`},{id:`b`,text:`You click through each setting in the portal`,why:`Manual configuration isn’t infrastructure as code.`},{id:`c`,text:`You copy settings from one resource to the next by hand`,why:`That’s manual configuration, not code.`},{id:`d`,text:`You describe the end state, and Azure works out how`,why:`Correct: declarative code says what should exist, and Azure Resource Manager works out the order.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Imperative code says how to deploy, step by step; declarative code says what should exist and lets Azure Resource Manager work out how.`},{id:`q10.4-02`,objectiveId:`10.4`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to deploy identical development, test, and production environments again and again with consistent results. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Manual setup in the Azure portal each time`,why:`Manual configuration makes identical, repeatable environments hard to guarantee.`},{id:`b`,text:`Infrastructure as code, such as Bicep`,why:`Correct: IaC keeps the definition in code, so you can deploy the same environment repeatedly with consistent results.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Advisor recommendations`,why:`Advisor suggests improvements; it doesn’t deploy environments.`},{id:`d`,text:`Resource locks on each environment`,why:`Locks prevent changes or deletion; they don’t build environments.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Infrastructure as code manages infrastructure through code and templates, so deployments are repeatable and consistent.`},{id:`q10.4-03`,objectiveId:`10.4`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each item to the infrastructure-as-code approach it uses. Each approach may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`An Azure CLI script of step-by-step commands`,why:`Scripts list the commands to run: imperative.`},{id:`i2`,text:`A Bicep file that describes the resources you want`,why:`Bicep describes the end state: declarative.`},{id:`i3`,text:`An ARM template in JSON`,why:`ARM templates describe what to deploy: declarative.`},{id:`i4`,text:`An Azure PowerShell script that creates resources in order`,why:`A script of commands in order is imperative.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Imperative`},{id:`t2`,text:`Declarative`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t2`,i4:`t1`},explanation:`Azure CLI and PowerShell scripts are imperative; ARM templates and Bicep are declarative.`},{id:`q10.4-04`,objectiveId:`10.4`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Learn describes Bicep files as ___: deploying the same file again gives the same result.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`imperative`,why:`Bicep is declarative, not imperative, and the property described here is idempotence.`},{id:`b`,text:`zone-redundant`,why:`Zone redundancy is about storage and service resiliency, not deployments.`},{id:`c`,text:`idempotent`,why:`Correct: Learn describes Bicep files as idempotent.`},{id:`d`,text:`asynchronous`,why:`Asynchronous describes timing, such as geo-replication, not repeatable deployments.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Idempotent means you can deploy the same file many times and get the same resources in the same state.`},{id:`q10.5-01`,objectiveId:`10.5`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A team manages Azure with the portal, the Azure CLI, and an SDK, and gets consistent results from all three. Which service does every one of their requests go through?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Arc`,why:`Azure Arc extends management to non-Azure resources; it isn’t the layer every request uses.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Cloud Shell`,why:`Cloud Shell is one place to run commands; requests still go through Resource Manager.`},{id:`c`,text:`Microsoft Entra Conditional Access`,why:`Conditional Access decides whether sign-ins are allowed; it isn’t the deployment and management service.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Resource Manager`,why:`Correct: ARM is the deployment and management service that every request goes through.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Because every tool uses the same Resource Manager API, you get consistent results from the portal, CLI, PowerShell, or templates.`},{id:`q10.5-02`,objectiveId:`10.5`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,covers:[`10.5|ARM vs ARM templates vs Bicep`],stem:`For each statement about Azure Resource Manager and its templates, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`ARM templates are JSON files.`,answer:!0,why:`ARM templates are JSON files that describe resources declaratively.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Bicep files deploy through a different service from ARM templates.`,answer:!1,why:`Bicep also deploys through Azure Resource Manager.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Azure validates an ARM template before deploying it.`,answer:!0,why:`Azure validates the template first, then orchestrates the deployment.`}],explanation:`ARM is the service; ARM templates (JSON) and Bicep are two languages for telling it what to deploy.`},{id:`q10.5-03`,objectiveId:`10.5`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two does Azure Resource Manager do? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Authenticates and authorizes each request`,why:`Correct: ARM authenticates and authorizes a request before sending it to the Azure service.`},{id:`b`,text:`Moves on-premises servers into Azure`,why:`Moving workloads into Azure is Azure Migrate.`},{id:`c`,text:`Queries collected log data`,why:`Querying logs is Log Analytics.`},{id:`d`,text:`Deploys resources in dependency order`,why:`Correct: ARM handles dependencies, and deploys in parallel where possible.`}],answer:[`a`,`d`],explanation:`Resource Manager is the management layer: it authenticates and authorizes requests, and orchestrates deployments in the right order.`},{id:`q10.5-04`,objectiveId:`10.5`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`___ is a simpler declarative language than JSON templates, and it also deploys through Azure Resource Manager.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure PowerShell`,why:`PowerShell runs imperative cmdlets; it isn’t a declarative template language.`},{id:`b`,text:`Bicep`,why:`Correct: Bicep is a simpler, more concise declarative language that deploys through ARM.`},{id:`c`,text:`The Azure CLI`,why:`The Azure CLI runs imperative Bash commands.`},{id:`d`,text:`Kusto Query Language`,why:`KQL is the language for querying logs in Log Analytics, not for deploying resources.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`ARM templates (JSON) and Bicep are both declarative ways to tell Resource Manager what to deploy; Bicep is simpler.`}],p=[{id:`q11.1-01`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`11.1|Microsoft Defender for Cloud`],stem:`A company wants one place with personalized recommendations to improve the reliability, performance, cost, and operational excellence of its Azure resources. Which service should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,why:`Defender for Cloud focuses only on security posture and threat protection, not cost or performance.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Correct: Advisor covers reliability, security, performance, operational excellence, and cost.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Service Health`,why:`Service Health reports on Azure outages and maintenance, not recommendations for your resources.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Policy`,why:`Azure Policy enforces your rules; it doesn’t recommend improvements.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Advisor covers five areas and includes Defender for Cloud’s recommendations; Defender for Cloud focuses only on security posture and threat protection.`},{id:`q11.1-02`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each Azure Advisor recommendation to its category. Each category may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i1`,text:`A change that will speed up your application`,why:`Speeding up applications is the performance category.`},{id:`i2`,text:`An improvement to your workflow and deployment process`,why:`Workflow and deployment improvements are operational excellence.`},{id:`i3`,text:`A way to reduce your Azure spending`,why:`Reducing spending is the cost category.`},{id:`i4`,text:`A vulnerability that could lead to a breach`,why:`Threats and vulnerabilities are the security category.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Performance`},{id:`t2`,text:`Operational excellence`},{id:`t3`,text:`Cost`},{id:`t4`,text:`Security`}],answer:{i1:`t1`,i2:`t2`,i3:`t3`,i4:`t4`},explanation:`Advisor’s categories are reliability, security, performance, operational excellence, and cost.`},{id:`q11.1-03`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure Advisor, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`You can postpone or dismiss an Advisor recommendation instead of acting on it.`,answer:!0,why:`Each recommendation can be acted on right away, postponed, or dismissed.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Azure Advisor is a separate app you install on your computer.`,answer:!1,why:`Advisor is built into the Azure portal.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Advisor can’t notify you about new recommendations; you have to check it.`,answer:!1,why:`Learn says you can set up notifications for new recommendations.`}],explanation:`Advisor lives in the Azure portal; each recommendation can be acted on, postponed, or dismissed, and you can be notified of new ones.`},{id:`q11.1-04`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,numeric:!0,stem:`Azure Advisor groups its recommendations into ___ categories.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`three`,why:`Advisor has more than three categories.`},{id:`b`,text:`seven`,why:`Seven is the number of defense-in-depth layers, not Advisor categories.`},{id:`c`,text:`four`,why:`Advisor has one more category than that.`},{id:`d`,text:`five`,why:`Correct: reliability, security, performance, operational excellence, and cost.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`The five Advisor categories are reliability, security, performance, operational excellence, and cost.`},{id:`q11.1-05`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`An administrator with several subscriptions wants to see Advisor recommendations for just one team’s resource group. What should they do?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Filter the Advisor dashboard by that resource group`,why:`Correct: the dashboard shows all your subscriptions, and you can filter by subscription, resource group, or service.`},{id:`b`,text:`Move the resource group to its own new subscription`,why:`There’s no need to move anything; you can filter the dashboard.`},{id:`c`,text:`Assign an Azure Policy to the resource group`,why:`Azure Policy enforces rules; it doesn’t filter Advisor’s view.`},{id:`d`,text:`Check Service Health for the region the group runs in`,why:`Service Health reports on Azure’s health, not Advisor recommendations.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`The Advisor dashboard covers all your subscriptions, and you can narrow it by subscription, resource group, or service.`},{id:`q11.1-06`,objectiveId:`11.1`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Advisor’s ___ recommendations flag configuration risks that could stop your applications from running.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`operational excellence`,why:`Operational excellence covers workflow and deployment improvements.`},{id:`b`,text:`performance`,why:`Performance covers changes that speed up your applications.`},{id:`c`,text:`reliability`,why:`Correct: the reliability category flags configuration risks that could stop your apps running.`},{id:`d`,text:`cost`,why:`Cost covers ways to reduce spending.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Reliability recommendations are about keeping your applications running.`},{id:`q11.2-01`,objectiveId:`11.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,covers:[`11.2|Azure status vs Service Health vs Resource Health`],stem:`An engineer hears about a widespread Azure outage in the news and wants the global picture across all Azure services and regions, not just the ones the company uses. Where should they look?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Service Health`,why:`Service Health focuses on the services and regions you use.`},{id:`b`,text:`Resource Health`,why:`Resource Health covers one specific resource.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure status`,why:`Correct: Azure status is the global view across all Azure services and regions.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor gives recommendations for your resources, not outage information.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Azure status covers all of Azure; Service Health covers the services and regions you use; Resource Health covers one specific resource.`},{id:`q11.2-02`,objectiveId:`11.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`One of a company’s virtual machines stopped responding. The team wants to know whether that VM is running normally and whether the problem is on Azure’s side or theirs. What should they check?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure status`,why:`Azure status is the global view; it doesn’t cover one of your VMs.`},{id:`b`,text:`Resource Health`,why:`Correct: Resource Health covers a single resource and whether an issue is on Azure’s side or yours.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor gives recommendations; it doesn’t report a resource’s current health.`},{id:`d`,text:`The pricing calculator`,why:`The pricing calculator estimates costs.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Resource Health zooms in on one resource, such as one VM, and tells you whether a problem is on Azure’s side or yours.`},{id:`q11.2-03`,objectiveId:`11.2`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to be notified automatically about Azure events that affect the specific Azure services and regions it uses. What should it set up?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Alerts in Azure Service Health`,why:`Correct: Service Health covers the services and regions you use, and you can set alerts for its events.`},{id:`b`,text:`A bookmark to the Azure status page`,why:`Azure status is the global view, and a bookmark doesn’t notify you.`},{id:`c`,text:`An Azure Advisor recommendation filter`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t report maintenance.`},{id:`d`,text:`A budget alert`,why:`Budget alerts are about spending.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`Service Health shows the outages, planned maintenance, and advisories that affect your environment, and can alert you automatically.`},{id:`q11.2-04`,objectiveId:`11.2`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure Service Health, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Service Health keeps historical alerts, so you can spot recurring trends.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says historical alerts are kept for this.`},{id:`s2`,text:`When an event affects your workloads, Service Health can link you to support.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says Service Health links you to support in that case.`},{id:`s3`,text:`The Azure status page shows only the services in your own subscriptions.`,answer:!1,why:`Azure status is the global view of all Azure services and regions.`}],explanation:`Service Health keeps history and connects you to support; Azure status is the global view for everyone.`},{id:`q11.2-05`,objectiveId:`11.2`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Service Health shows the outages, planned maintenance, and ___ that affect the services and regions you use.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`cost recommendations`,why:`Cost recommendations come from Azure Advisor.`},{id:`b`,text:`secure scores`,why:`The secure score comes from Microsoft Defender for Cloud.`},{id:`c`,text:`budget forecasts`,why:`Budgets and forecasts are in Microsoft Cost Management.`},{id:`d`,text:`health advisories`,why:`Correct: Service Health shows outages, planned maintenance, and health advisories.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Service Health reports three kinds of events for your environment: outages, planned maintenance, and health advisories.`},{id:`q11.3-01`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`11.3|Azure Monitor vs Service Health`],stem:`A company wants to be notified when the CPU usage of its own VMs stays high, based on metrics collected from those VMs. What should it use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Azure Service Health`,why:`Service Health reports on the health of Azure services, not your VMs’ own metrics.`},{id:`b`,text:`Azure Advisor recommendations`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t alert on live metrics.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure status`,why:`Azure status is the global health of Azure, not your resources.`},{id:`d`,text:`An Azure Monitor alert`,why:`Correct: Azure Monitor alerts fire when a condition you define on your resources’ metrics or logs is met.`}],answer:`d`,explanation:`Azure Monitor tracks the performance and logs of your own resources and apps; Service Health reports on the health of the Azure services you use.`},{id:`q11.3-02`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`Several Azure Monitor alert rules should all notify the same on-call team and trigger the same follow-up steps. What should they share?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`An action group`,why:`Correct: an action group controls who gets notified and what happens next, and alert rules can share it.`},{id:`b`,text:`A resource group for all the alert rules`,why:`A resource group organizes resources; it doesn’t decide who is notified.`},{id:`c`,text:`A single Log Analytics query`,why:`A query can be an alert condition, but it doesn’t decide who is notified.`},{id:`d`,text:`A Service Health alert`,why:`Service Health alerts cover Azure’s own service health, not your alert rules’ notifications.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`An alert rule sets the condition; an action group controls who is notified and what happens next.`},{id:`q11.3-03`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!0,source:`framing`,covers:[`11.3|Log Analytics vs Application Insights`],stem:`An engineer wants to write a query that returns every error logged in the last hour across the company’s collected log data. Which tool should they use?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Application Insights`,why:`Application Insights collects performance and usage data from web apps; querying the collected logs is Log Analytics.`},{id:`b`,text:`Resource Health`,why:`Resource Health reports whether one resource is healthy.`},{id:`c`,text:`Log Analytics`,why:`Correct: Log Analytics is where you write and run queries against collected log data.`},{id:`d`,text:`Azure Advisor`,why:`Advisor recommends improvements; it doesn’t query logs.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Log Analytics is where you query collected log data; Application Insights collects performance and usage data from web applications.`},{id:`q11.3-04`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!0,source:`learn`,stem:`A company wants to check regularly whether its public web app is reachable and responding. Which Azure Monitor feature can run availability tests?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Log Analytics`,why:`Log Analytics queries data that’s already collected; it doesn’t run availability tests.`},{id:`b`,text:`Application Insights`,why:`Correct: Application Insights monitors web apps and can run availability tests.`},{id:`c`,text:`Azure status`,why:`Azure status reports on Azure itself, not your web app.`},{id:`d`,text:`An Azure Monitor action group`,why:`An action group decides who is notified; it doesn’t test anything.`}],answer:`b`,explanation:`Application Insights is the Azure Monitor feature for web applications, including availability tests.`},{id:`q11.3-05`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`yesno`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`For each statement about Azure Monitor, select Yes if it is true. Otherwise, select No.`,statements:[{id:`s1`,text:`Azure Monitor can collect data from on-premises and other clouds, not only from Azure.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says it collects data from resources in Azure, on-premises, or other clouds.`},{id:`s2`,text:`Azure Monitor stores the logs and metrics it collects in a central place.`,answer:!0,why:`Learn says Azure Monitor stores the data centrally and makes it available through dashboards, queries, and alerts.`},{id:`s3`,text:`Log Analytics is a separate app you install outside the Azure portal.`,answer:!1,why:`Log Analytics is a tool in the Azure portal.`}],explanation:`Azure Monitor gathers data from Azure, on-premises, and other clouds and stores it centrally; Log Analytics, where you query it, is in the portal.`},{id:`q11.3-06`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`multiple`,difficulty:`medium`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which two does Application Insights track for a web application? Select two.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`Planned maintenance in Azure regions`,why:`Planned maintenance is reported by Service Health.`},{id:`b`,text:`Request rates and response times`,why:`Correct: Application Insights tracks request rates and response times.`},{id:`c`,text:`The subscription’s secure score`,why:`The secure score comes from Defender for Cloud.`},{id:`d`,text:`Failures`,why:`Correct: Application Insights tracks failures, along with dependencies, page loads, and users.`}],answer:[`b`,`d`],explanation:`Application Insights tracks request rates, response times, failures, dependencies, page loads, and users.`},{id:`q11.3-07`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`completion`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`In Azure Monitor, an alert ___ sets the condition that triggers an alert.`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`rule`,why:`Correct: an alert rule sets the condition.`},{id:`b`,text:`group`,why:`An action group decides who is notified, not the condition.`},{id:`c`,text:`lock`,why:`Locks protect resources from deletion or changes.`},{id:`d`,text:`tag`,why:`Tags are name-value metadata on resources.`}],answer:`a`,explanation:`An alert rule sets the condition; an action group controls who is notified and what happens next.`},{id:`q11.3-08`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`single`,difficulty:`easy`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Which statement best describes Azure Monitor?`,options:[{id:`a`,text:`A service that gives personalized best-practice recommendations for your resources`,why:`That describes Azure Advisor.`},{id:`b`,text:`A global view of the health of every Azure region`,why:`That describes Azure status.`},{id:`c`,text:`A platform to collect, analyze, and act on data from your resources and apps`,why:`Correct: Azure Monitor collects logs and metrics, stores them centrally, and makes them available through dashboards, queries, and alerts.`},{id:`d`,text:`A tool that estimates what resources will cost before you deploy them`,why:`That describes the pricing calculator.`}],answer:`c`,explanation:`Azure Monitor gathers logs and metrics from applications, operating systems, and network layers, and makes them available for analysis and alerts.`},{id:`q11.3-09`,objectiveId:`11.3`,type:`matching`,difficulty:`hard`,scenario:!1,source:`learn`,stem:`Match each task to the Azure Monitor feature that does it. Each feature may be used once, more than once, or not at all.`,items:[{id:`i2`,text:`Track calls from a web app to the services it depends on`,why:`Application Insights tracks a web app’s dependencies.`},{id:`i3`,text:`Notify someone when a log search finds a match`,why:`That is a log-based Azure Monitor alert.`},{id:`i4`,text:`See how many users load each page of a web app`,why:`Application Insights tracks page loads and users.`}],targets:[{id:`t1`,text:`Log Analytics`},{id:`t2`,text:`Azure Monitor alerts`},{id:`t3`,text:`Application Insights`}],answer:{i2:`t3`,i3:`t2`,i4:`t3`},explanation:`Alerts notify when a condition is met (including log-based conditions), Application Insights watches web app performance and usage, and Log Analytics is for querying data.`}],m=[...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d,...f,...p];new Map(m.map(e=>[e.id,e]));export{m as t};